#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""devloop-stock-take: the stock-take of milestone 1 in docs/plan.md.

Reads docs/stock-take.tsv and docs/defects.md, and docs/roadmap.md with its
archives docs/roadmap-archive-<n>.md for the heads, the run lines and the
proofs; checks every anchor in them, computes the state of every thing and
of every defect from the records, the blocks and the git history, and lists
every line of the search set that no record covers. It stores nothing: the table and the blocks hold facts only,
and every state is computed on every run, because a stored state is an
asserted state ("Never assert state — query it", docs/skill-conventions.md);
a block of docs/defects.md names its state, and the tool holds that against
what it computes.

Usage
    scripts/devloop-stock-take                  the whole table
    scripts/devloop-stock-take --only PATH ...  coverage limited to files under
                                                the given paths
    scripts/devloop-stock-take --self-test      builds a temporary repository
                                                and produces every red outcome
                                                once, each beside its green
                                                counterpart; then reads every
                                                message the tool rejects,
                                                refuses or answers with off
                                                its own source and prints the
                                                ones no case asserted. The
                                                lines of the report are not
                                                in that count

Running the tool at all parses the file, so the self-test is the syntax check.
python3 -m py_compile is not needed and not to be used: it leaves bytecode
under scripts/__pycache__/, which the check over untracked files under bin/ and
scripts/ then reports, and a note at that check alone has failed to prevent it
twice, because the check is read only once it is red.

The counts by site file - things as straight paths, branches and other kinds,
the note forms under C and not-a-thing records - are printed so that a
report copies them rather than counting by hand. So are the roots of the
unattended mode and the skills it reaches, computed on every run, and the
defects by section of docs/defects.md, the states counted per section.

Exit code 0: nothing broken, nothing uncovered. 1: lines of the search set are
uncovered, or a unit has no straight path. 2: a record is broken, an anchor
does not stand where its record says, a run under the version this branch
introduces was recorded before a later commit of the branch changed a line of
its thing, a block of docs/defects.md is rejected or names a state the tool
does not compute, a defect's closing names a proof that does not hold, a
block that stood in docs/defects.md at the branch point is gone, renamed or
carries another Should with no line of the fixed form added to
docs/roadmap.md, or such a line names what the branch did not do, an archive
of the roadmap that stood at the branch point is changed, renamed or removed,
or an archive the branch adds is not docs/roadmap.md at the branch point byte
for byte under the next free number (section F), or the repository is one the
tool refuses (a shallow clone).

It needs python3 and git and nothing else, and no GNU tool: it runs on macOS.

This tool is not one of the checks under "Before a handover, run these" in
docs/skill-conventions.md. Nearly every change to a skill touches an anchored
line, so it would answer in the ordinary case, and "Before you change
anything, run this" says what becomes of such a check: read once, explained
away, skipped. It runs where the table is used, and after every rebase of the
branch that fills the table. Since 29 September 2026 it also runs, with its
self-test, on every pull request into main, in .github/workflows/stock-take.yml
against the merged state, and that run is the check main requires before a
merge - a setting of the repository, read off the platform and standing in no
file; docs/roadmap.md, the entry of 29 September 2026 on the merge gate, has
both halves, and the entry of 30 September 2026 on the audit records the
first run.


A. WHAT GOES IN

- Every branch of the twelve skills, all files in each skill directory, and
  the straight path of every unit of those files.
- Every branch of the text under shared/, and the straight path of every unit.
- Every branch of the output style under output-styles/, and the straight
  path of every unit, since 15 October 2026: the one file there is text the
  model reads in every session in which the plugin is enabled, a text no
  skill inserts and none owns, of kind output style.
- Every outcome of each hook, and hooks.json as one thing. A hook's outcomes
  are what it produces (passes, each with the condition that produced it, or
  blocks with a given message), not its exit statements.
- Every outcome of every program under bin/ and scripts/, this tool included.
  For hooks and programs alike, an outcome is what the program produces - a
  pass, a block, an exit code, an output - never an internal condition. An
  internal condition is "part of" the outcome it leads to. This tool's
  outcomes are the cases of its self-test.
- Every outcome of every workflow under .github/workflows/, each a thing of
  kind program outcome: what the platform's run of it produces - the check
  green or red, a refusal before the tool runs, a run cancelled or stopped -
  never a step's internal condition. A workflow is a program the platform
  runs on every pull request, as a hook is one the harness runs on every tool
  call, so a fix standing in one is built; decided on 30 September 2026, in
  the roadmap entry of that date on the audit.
- Both outcomes of every command under "Before a handover, run these" and of
  the command under "Before you change anything, run this": the answer its
  section calls green, and any other. The green form is the record's note.
  Where the section does not say what green looks like, the note says so;
  whoever writes the record does not supply one.
- The entries under "Named, not built as skills" in docs/roadmap.md that the
  section does not record as done inside another skill or as a stage, each a
  thing of kind named skill; how many is read off the tool's output, not off
  this header. The others are not things of their own: each name stands as a
  note on the row of the thing that does its work. The side paths under "What
  is missing" in README.md are among the named things and get no second row.
- Every defect, as a block of docs/defects.md, open or closed, section F:
  since 2 November 2026, decided by the person on that day, the roadmap entry
  of that date on the defects file. Until then a defect was a row of the
  table, sited on the line of docs/roadmap.md that named it, every defect an
  entry under "Known gaps" named, the three defects and seven mechanisms of
  the Pocock comparison and the remaining points of "The order of work, and
  why" included, the grilling standing as part of the defect an earlier entry
  measured first and the remaining points being two of the three defects.
  Whether a defect is still open is computed, never judged while writing.
- Trigger, bench and cost belong to the thing or the defect, as how it could
  be walked, not to a run. Each with its anchor, where the roadmap names
  them.
- Every finding made while reading: a line that is wrong, or a place where
  something is missing. A finding is a defect and is written as a block of
  docs/defects.md, with Fix none, since 2 November 2026; until then it was a
  row of the table of type finding, which an order moved into a dated entry
  and replaced by a defect thing. A finding in a document - docs/roadmap.md,
  docs/plan.md, docs/skill-conventions.md, docs/rationale.md -, in a line of
  docs/stock-take.tsv or in a field of a block of docs/defects.md that the
  same branch corrects, puts right in its own entry or mends gets no block,
  since such a finding would read as not built where nothing is left to do;
  the entry names it with its place, the wrong text and the right one. The
  line of the table and the field of a block since 3 November 2026, decided
  by the person on that day, the roadmap entry of that date on the follow-up
  to the audit, for the same reason. A finding in the code, the skills, the
  shared texts, the hooks, the programs or the workflow gets a block in
  every case.

B. WHAT A BRANCH IS, AND WHAT A UNIT IS

A branch: every outcome of a condition the run reads at run time and after
which it does or says something different. Each outcome of each condition
once, never each path through several conditions.

An offer is a condition. Its answers are outcomes wherever they lead to
different action, even where the text says the run carries on either way:
setup-checks offers to take the workflow file back out, and a yes deletes a
file while a no leaves it in the repository. Where the text does not say what
a declined offer leads to, the thing stands with an empty evidence, which
shows the gap by itself; that silence alone is not a finding. The offers are
found with the search the check for offers under "Before a handover, run
these" uses, and every hit is walked against this rule.

A unit: what a heading of any level opens, from that heading to the line
before the next heading of any level. The first unit of a file starts at line
1, so the frontmatter belongs to it: allowed-tools is what the insert lines
run under. A file without a heading is one unit. A line inside a fenced code
block is never a heading, neither for the search set nor for the boundaries of
a unit: skills/untangle-idea/SKILL.md carries a template with headings inside
a fence and, further down, a heading of the skill in the same words. An insert
line (!`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/devloop-text NAME`) brings shared/NAME.md
into the range of its unit, followed once per file.

Every unit has one thing for its straight path - kind skill branch in a skill,
shared text under shared/, output style under output-styles/ - named
<source> / <unit> / straight path and anchored at the unit's heading. A
sentence without a condition in the unit is "part of" it; every condition
keeps its own things. A unit whose heading cannot carry an anchor anchors its
straight path on the nearest anchorable line inside the unit; a unit with no
anchorable line at all is listed with the unanchorable lines. Every unit of a
file under skills/, shared/ or output-styles/ has exactly one straight path,
and the tool checks it: a unit without one is listed and counts as uncovered,
a second one is a broken record.

Branches are read from the files on disk, never from the expanded text. A
branch in inserted text is a row of its file under shared/, once. Where the
shared text hands the decision to the skill (shared/how-to-ask.md does so for
the case with nobody there), the branch stands only at the skill.

Which skills the unattended mode reaches is computed, never read off a note.
A root is a thing carrying the note "enters the unattended mode"; the tool
takes every thing carrying it as a root, and with no root it says so and does
not compute the set. From the roots it follows every call note claimed as
reached, and every call in shared text, until nothing changes: a skill is
reached when a call to it is claimed as reached and stands in a skill that is
itself reached, and a call inside shared text counts for every skill that
inserts that file. The roots and the reached set are printed on every run, so
a missing or a wrong root is seen there and not only in its consequences. Two
things carry the note since 22 September 2026: build-work / Unattended mode /
straight path, where the direct route writes the mark, and plan-work / With
nobody there / straight path, where planning runs alone from the end of Stage
1. An order that finds another entry adds the note there.

Reach is per skill, and the boundary is per skill too. plan-work is reached
only from the end of Stage 1: its preamble and Stage 1 stay with the user,
and the line "Whether it does is decided once, at the end of Stage 1" draws
the boundary. In every skill the mode reaches, every point beyond that
boundary where the skill hands the user something to do or asks something
gets a row for the case with nobody there, even where the skill does not say
what happens then; before the boundary nothing is added, since the mode never
gets there. A reached skill with neither a section of its own for the case
nor shared/how-to-ask.md inserted gets an empty evidence for that row. Where
the skill does not say what happens then, the evidence stays empty and the
note carries "outcome with nobody there not stated". Where two lines of a
skill disagree about where the boundary runs, that is a finding, and the
place counts as reached while it stands: an undetermined row that turns out
to be unreachable is visible and can be dropped, a gap never written down is
not. Where a reached skill draws no boundary at all - no line of its own
saying from where it runs alone, and no inserted text saying it - the whole
skill counts as reached, and the report of the order that inventories it says
which search came back empty for that skill. build-work, setup-checks and
plan-work each draw theirs under a "With nobody there" section of their own,
so this rule asks for no retrofit.

A line that says what a run does with nobody there is a condition like any
other and gets its thing, whether or not the unattended mode reaches the
skill. A thing with an empty evidence is added for a handover or a question
the skill leaves unanswered only where the unattended mode reaches the skill.

A call to another skill is not a thing of its own. It stands as a note, in one
of the forms under C, on the thing whose outcome makes the call. Which form
follows from the reached set: in a reached skill, "reached per" where the
call stands on a path the mode takes, and "not reached per" where it stands
before the skill's boundary or on a path the mode does not take, naming the
thing that draws the boundary; in a skill nothing reaches, the third form,
which says so. In shared text the call stands alone, without a claim, because
the file stands in several skills and the tool derives the claim from the
skills that insert it. Calls are
looked for under shared/ as well as under skills/, because inserted text is
the skill's text: a search over skills/ alone missed "call `setup-checks`" in
shared/checks-owner.md, inserted into build-work and diagnose-bug, on 22
September 2026. A call phrased without "run" or "call" before the name, such
as "the next stage is `plan-work`", is a call all the same. A branch one
skill describes and another decides stands once, where it is decided; the
other place goes in the note.

C. RECORDS

docs/stock-take.tsv: tab-separated, one record per line, the first line the
header of the columns below. No tab in any field, no leading or trailing
whitespace in an anchor, no absolute local path anywhere. Every record carries
exactly the columns of the header; a tab that an editor turned into spaces
makes the record one field short, and the record is rejected rather than read.
Fields a record type does not use stay empty; a required field that is empty,
an unknown type or kind, or a filled field the type does not use rejects the
record. A rejected record covers nothing.

Columns, in this order:
    type name kind file anchor evidence_file evidence_anchor note date
    version commit source run_file run_anchor trigger_file trigger_anchor
    bench_file bench_anchor cost_file cost_anchor

An anchor is a file and a verbatim substring of one line of it, 20 to 80
characters, occurring exactly once in that file. An anchor may be shorter than
20 characters where it is a whole line, and it must still occur exactly once.
One found more than once is rejected with the lines it stands on named, since
12 October 2026; until then the message said only that it was found more than
once.
No line numbers anywhere: the opening of docs/plan.md records that line
numbers drifted by the next pull request while the text did not.

Three record types, since 2 November 2026; until then a fourth, finding,
and a ninth kind of thing, defect, both of which the blocks of docs/defects.md
replaced, section F:

- thing: name (unique among things), kind, file + anchor (the site: where it
  stands or is named), evidence_file + evidence_anchor (what carries its
  state; may be empty), note, and optionally trigger, bench and cost, each a
  file + anchor pair. Kinds: skill branch, shared text, output style, hook
  outcome, hook registration, program outcome, check command, named skill.
  For a branch on disk, site and evidence are usually the same line. For a
  named skill, the note carries the search by what it does, not only by its
  name, that came back empty. For a check command, the note carries the green
  form.
- run, keyed to a thing by its name or to a defect by its title: date
  (YYYY-MM-DD), the version it ran or the commit it ran, source (entry: the
  roadmap entry names it; log: read from the session log of that run; none:
  not recorded), run_file + run_anchor (the line that records the run, in
  docs/roadmap.md or in the archive docs/roadmap-archive-<n>.md that holds
  the entry: the roadmap is the dated report in docs/roadmap.md and its
  archives, section F). A run that stands on the row of a
  shared text or a branch counts for no defect by itself, since the rule of
  section D may undercount and never overcount: a run enters a defect's row
  once the session log of that run has been read for whether it met exactly
  the defect's case, with the version the run ran under, and whoever
  evaluates a run enters it so on the row of every defect whose case it met;
  decided by the person on 20 October 2026, the roadmap entry of that date.
  A commit enters a run record only where
  the entry says the run ran that commit; a commit named for another reason,
  such as the one that built a fix, is not a run's version. A run's commit
  must be an ancestor of origin/main, and the tool checks it: that rejects a
  bench's commits, which do not resolve here, and a commit that exists only on
  a branch, which a squash merge removes. A version, by contrast, is resolved
  on the history of the checkout, so a run recorded on a branch under the
  version its merge will introduce counts before the merge and after it; such
  a run is recorded only at the branch's final state, and the tool holds it
  to that since 14 October 2026, below. A version whose
  introducing commit is not on origin/main is the branch's own, and a squash
  merge lands only the version the branch ends on: a run under such a version
  is a broken record unless the version is the one .claude-plugin/plugin.json
  carries in the working tree, so that a branch which raised the version
  twice is red before its merge, where the runs under its first version can
  still be re-anchored to its last, and not only on main after it. Nineteen
  runs of pull request #141 went that way on 29 September 2026: recorded
  under 0.116.0 in the branch's first order, raised over to 0.116.1 in its
  second, rejected on main as never introduced, and re-anchored to 0.116.1
  the same day, since the shipped directories had not changed between the two
  raises. Without origin/main the tool cannot tell a branch's version from
  main's and does not check this.
  The check against a run recorded too early, since 14 October 2026: where
  the version .claude-plugin/plugin.json carries in the working tree was
  introduced on this branch - its commit not on origin/main - every run under
  it is held, for every line its thing stands on, against the commit that
  brought its row into the table, read with git blame of the table. A row
  whose commit does not contain the last change to such a line is a broken
  record, the row's commit, the line and the change's commit named. The
  check main requires goes red on the pull request, whose run reads the
  branch with its full history on refs/pull/N/merge; on main after the squash
  merge nothing of this can be seen, every line blaming to the one commit.
  Twenty-four runs of pull request #171 went that way: recorded on 12 and 13
  October 2026 on the branch, lines of their things changed by later commits
  of it, counted on main against a03a720, and taken out of the table on 14
  October 2026. Not seen: a row re-anchored after the change carries the
  later commit and passes, whenever its run was made; a run made before a
  change and recorded in the one commit with it passes, the row's commit
  being the change's; and with origin/main not fetched the check runs on
  main as well, where every row's commit is the squash commit and contains
  everything, so nothing is reported there. The run's date is not held
  against the day of the change, and was for a day: a run's date is the day
  its session log opens, in the log's own timezone, and a commit's day is
  read in its author's, so a session opened before midnight that commits
  after it and runs after the commit carries the earlier day on the run and
  the later on the change, and a test on the dates reports a run that was
  not early.
  A run whose thing is rejected is rejected with it, with a message naming
  the thing's line, and covers nothing.
- not-a-thing: file + anchor of a line from the search set judged not to be a
  branch, and a note that starts with one reason from the list below.

Thing names take the form <source> / <unit> / <outcome>, the parts separated
by " / ". The source: the skill's name where the site is that skill's
SKILL.md, and the file's path from the repository root everywhere else -
skills/build-prototype/LOGIC.md, shared/how-to-ask.md, hooks/stop-checks.sh,
bin/devloop-text - so one form covers everything that is not a SKILL.md. The
unit: the heading of the unit the site lies in, without its # marks. Where
the site lies in a file that has no units - a hook, hooks.json, a program
under bin/ or scripts/, a markdown file without a heading - it is "whole
file". Where the site lies in a file with headings that is not a unit file,
it is the heading of the section the site stands under, written as the file
writes it: "Before you change anything, run this" and "Before a handover, run
these" in docs/skill-conventions.md, "Named, not built as skills" in
docs/roadmap.md. Where that heading is not unique in its file, the unit part
carries the headings enclosing it before it, joined by " > ", as many as it
takes to be unique: two headings alike inside one file collide exactly as two
files do. Where even the whole chain collides, the two units cannot be told
apart by their text; that is recorded as a finding rather than a name
invented, and the tool lists the unit among those without a straight path,
since none can be named. The outcome - in English, and unique. The tool
rejects a name without exactly those three parts, a source part that does not
match the site of any thing, and a unit part that does not match the site's
file: the tool derives the unit part for every site - "whole file" for a file
that is not markdown, the heading or its chain for every markdown file, in
docs/ as under skills/ - so an order that names a hook, a check command or a
named skill cannot drift from the one that names them later. A name carries
no "; ", because
the note forms below use it as their separator and a "part of:" note names a
thing up to it. For a named skill the third part is the skill's name, and
that is what is held against skills/.

A thing whose name no longer says what the thing says is renamed, where no
run hangs on it; where runs hang on it, it keeps its name, since the name is
what its runs are keyed by, and the note says what holds now. Two things
were renamed so on 9 October 2026, the registration in hooks.json and
start condition 4 of build-work, both with no run; the note of a renamed
thing names the old name.

A thing's note carries each of these forms exactly, separated from anything
else in the note by "; ":
- calls <skill>; with nobody there: reached per <thing name> - only in a
  skill the mode reaches
- calls <skill>; with nobody there: not reached per <thing name> - only in a
  skill the mode reaches, where the call stands before the boundary or on a
  path the mode does not take
- calls <skill>; with nobody there: not stated per <thing name> - only in a
  skill the mode reaches, and only where the thing it names carries the
  last form below
- calls <skill>; with nobody there: the mode does not reach this skill -
  only in a skill nothing reaches
- calls <skill> - alone, only on a thing under shared/; a claim after it is
  rejected there, since the tool derives it
- enters the unattended mode - a root of the computed set; on a record that
  is not a thing, or on a thing whose site is not under skills/, it is
  rejected
- does the work of <named skill> - a name from the table under "Named, not
  built as skills" in docs/roadmap.md
- outcome with nobody there not stated
<skill> is a directory under skills/. The thing named after "per" is the one
whose line shows whether the unattended mode takes the path the call stands
on. Where a reached skill draws no boundary, a call inside it takes "reached
per" naming the thing through which the reached set arrives at that skill -
the tool prints that thing beside every reached skill. A segment of the note
that starts like one of these forms and does not carry it exactly rejects the
record, and so does a reach claim the computed set does not carry.

A not-a-thing record starts its note with one reason from this list, separated
from anything else in the note by "; ", and the tool rejects any other:
- part of: <thing name> - the line belongs to a thing anchored on another
  line: the straight path of its unit, or a branch; the name must be a thing's
- rationale - it explains why, or where a rule came from, and does not direct
  the run
- quoted - it quotes another file or an earlier wording
- template - it is part of a template or an example the run writes or shows
Until 2 November 2026 a fifth reason, no defect, marked an entry head under
"Known gaps" whose entry named no defect; the heads left the search set with
the status lines that day, section E.

A status line of docs/roadmap.md that records something built and no defect
- "Built: a hook for permission prompts" say - was recorded as "part of" the
thing it records, runs or no runs, since 23 October 2026, and such a record
stayed when the line left the search set on 2 November 2026, since taking it
out changes what the thing's runs are held against. On 4 November 2026 the
entries that carry such lines moved to docs/roadmap-archive-1.md, every line
of which git blame attributes to the commit that created the file, so that a
record re-pointed there would have held every run of its thing to the day of
the archive: the 34 records of that form went instead, decided in the order
of that day, the roadmap entry of that date on the archive, which names each
and what it did to the state of its thing - one thing Walked whose runs had
failed on such a line alone, the rest as they stood. Since that day a line
of docs/roadmap.md or of an archive counts for no run, as it counts for no
defect since 2 November 2026; no line of an archive is in any search set.
Until 4 November 2026 such a line blamed to the commit of its own entry,
appended and left as written, or to a correction in place, as an audit makes
one, after which the thing's runs counted again from the first version that
carries the correction: an undercount, which section D allows, never an
overcount. Until 23 October 2026 this section said a status line was never
recorded as part of a thing whose runs are to count, since a line of the
roadmap was changed by every later entry, and named a defect as the thing
whose runs are not kept; the entry of 9 October 2026 on the behaviour with
nobody there moved its status line on the hook for permission prompts from
that hook's deny onto the registration in hooks.json for that reason. Since
20 October 2026 defects carry runs of benches, and the audit of 23 October
2026 found four rows standing against the old rule, the status lines on the
install guard's pass built in 4bad07e, on the hook for permission prompts,
on the rule for the classifier and on the guard on the identity of a commit,
each part of a thing with runs; they are rows of this form. Until 2 November
2026 a status line that named a defect was covered by that defect, whose
site it was.

D. HOW STATES ARE COMPUTED

For a thing, off its evidence; for a defect, off the Fix of its block, which
is its evidence, and off the closing its Status carries.

- Undetermined: the evidence is empty, or a defect's Fix is "not determined".
  A repair moves the evidence onto the line that carries it, in the change
  that lands the repair - a case of the self-test, a line of a skill, a line
  of the conventions - since a table or a block reading "Recorded, not built"
  for something now built is the state this tool exists to end.
- Recorded, not built: the evidence lies under docs/ or in README.md, and the
  kind is not check command; or a defect's Fix is "none", which says that
  nothing is built.
- Built: the evidence lies under skills/, shared/, output-styles/, hooks/,
  bin/, scripts/ or .github/, or the kind is check command, or the evidence
  lies under one of the two
  check headings of docs/skill-conventions.md, "Before you change anything,
  run this" and "Before a handover, run these". The line is drawn around
  those two sections and not around the whole file because they are run -
  somebody executes them before a handover - while the rest of that file is
  a rule for whoever writes a skill, not a mechanism a run meets: a defect
  answered only by a written convention stays recorded, and that is right.
  The enclosing heading is derived from the evidence line, never claimed, so
  an anchor a line away from a check reads the same as one on it.
- Walked: built, and at least one of its runs counts.
- Built, never walked: built, and no run counts. Runs that do not count stay
  in the output, each with the reason.
- Closed: the Status of a defect's block carries its closing with one of
  three reasons and the proof that reason asks for, and the tool has
  confirmed the proof on this run with its own means for anchors; since 20
  October 2026, decided by the person that day, the roadmap entry of that
  date, when the closing stood on a line of docs/roadmap.md that was the
  defect's evidence, and since 2 November 2026 in the block. The closing
  takes one of these three forms, whole on one physical line, the file a path
  from the repository root in backticks and the text in double quotes, 20 to
  80 characters, no tab and no double quote in it:
    Closed, lapsed: `FILE` no longer carries "TEXT", removed at `COMMIT`.
    Closed, lapsed: `FILE` no longer carries "TEXT".
    Closed, corrected in the document: `FILE` carries "TEXT".
    Closed, left on purpose: decided in `FILE` at "TEXT".
  Lapsed: what the defect describes is gone or works otherwise, so that its
  should has no object; the proof is a file and a text that stood in it and
  stands there no more, and where it can be found the commit that removed the
  place. The tool reads that the text is not in the file now, that it stood in
  the file at some commit of this history (git log -S), and where a commit is
  named, that it resolves, that the file at that commit does not carry the
  text and that the file at its first parent does. Corrected in the document:
  the wrong sentence itself was the defect and has been corrected where it
  stood; the proof is the file and the text of the corrected sentence, which
  the tool reads as an anchor, found exactly once. This reason holds only
  where the sentence itself was the defect: a defect that a written
  convention answers is not closed by it and stays recorded, as the paragraph
  on the two check headings above says. Left on purpose: the repository says,
  with a reason, that it is not built; the proof is the line that decides it,
  file and text, read as an anchor found exactly once. Where the file of the
  proof is docs/roadmap.md, an archive docs/roadmap-archive-<n>.md of it or
  docs/defects.md, the lines that carry a closing form quoting the text - the
  closing line of the entry that closed the defect, the Status of its block -
  are left out of the count. A Status that
  opens with Closed and carries none of the three forms, or a proof the tool
  cannot confirm, rejects the block as broken, the message naming what
  failed, and the defect is not closed; a command standing in the text of
  the roadmap or of a block is never run. A closed defect cannot be walked,
  so a run on it is a broken record as on a thing that is not built. The
  tool counts closed defects apart from every other state and lists them in
  a list of their own, under CLOSED DEFECTS, and in no list of open things.

A block names its state in its Status, in these words or as a closing, and
the tool rejects a block whose Status names another state than the one it
computes: the block is a record of a fact, the Fix, and the state is read
off the fact, never off the block.

A run counts when the version it ran already contains the last change to
every line the thing stands on. Those lines are: for a straight path, every
line of its unit from its heading to the line before the next heading, and
every line of every file its insert lines bring in; for a branch, its site
line and its evidence line; for either, every line recorded as "part of" it;
for a defect, the Fix line of its block and no other, since 2 November 2026
- until then the line of the roadmap that named it and the lines recorded
as part of it as well, so that a block can move between the sections of
docs/defects.md and a later decision can change its Should without a run
being lost, and so that a line of the roadmap, which a later entry never
changes, decides nothing about a run.
git blame names the commit of each line's last change, and the commit that
introduced the version number into .claude-plugin/plugin.json must have every
one of them as an ancestor, itself included. That commit and no later one,
because a version's content is fixed only if every change to the shipped
directories raised the number; where that does not hold, it is the one commit
every copy of the version is certain to contain. Every line, because
otherwise a change to a unit's content would go unseen and a run on the old
content would count for the new: the rule can undercount and must never
overcount. Where the run carries a commit instead, that commit must have every
blamed commit as an ancestor, itself included.

Blame is plain: a moved line counts as changed, because where a line stands
is part of what it decides (a check moved to a place where its red means
something else, a rule moved onto another route). What copy detection would
attribute the line to (git blame -C -C) is printed as information beside a run
that does not count, never as a state.

A check command runs in this repository, not from the installed copy, and its
run is held against a version all the same, the one .claude-plugin/plugin.json
carried in the tree it ran in: recorded on a branch under the version that
branch introduces, the run counts there and keeps counting after the squash,
which a commit could not do. Until 26 September 2026 a check command's run was
held against the commit date of its line instead, and a squash merge re-dates
that line. A run whose version is not recorded does not count. An uncommitted
line counts as changed after every run.

On a shallow clone the tool refuses to work: git blame there reports every
line as the boundary commit.

E. THE SEARCH SET, FOR COVERAGE

- In every file under skills/, shared/ and output-styles/: every line
  holding one of the words if, where, when, whenever, wherever, unless,
  otherwise, either, else, before, as whole words in either case; every
  heading outside a fenced code block; every numbered item; every bold list
  head; every bold paragraph head; every table row except the header row and
  the separator row.
- In every file under hooks/: every line holding if, elif, else, case or exit
  as whole words, and every line that calls, at the place of a command, a
  function of the file that ends the program; the rule two items below.
- Under bin/ and scripts/: in a shell program the same five words and the
  same calls; in a python program if, elif, raise, exit, except - a caught
  error is a branch, and this tool's own red outcomes sit there. Not else and
  not return: an else line on its own is never anchorable and its branch is
  anchored at its if, and a return is a function's straight path. The
  language is read off the first line.
- Under .github/workflows/: the five shell words, and every line holding
  run:, branches:, timeout-minutes: or cancel-in-progress:, the keys that
  decide what a workflow runs and when. A step's run: line that repeats
  another's opening, as the tool's step does the self-test's, can carry no
  unique anchor and leaves the set as any such line does.
- In none of these files a comment line: a line whose first character other
  than a space or a tab is #, and in a python program every line of a string
  that stands as a statement of its own, a docstring. A code line with a
  comment behind it stays a code line. Until 12 October 2026 such a line
  stood in the set as code did, and each one holding a search word carried a
  record saying so.
- An exit through a function, in a shell file under hooks/, bin/ or scripts/.
  A function ends the program when a line of its body other than a comment
  line holds the word exit or calls a function that ends the program. Every
  line that calls such a function at the place of a command - at the start of
  the line, or after ;, &&, ||, |, !, (, {, the parenthesis that closes a
  case pattern, then, do or else - stands in the set as an exit does, inside
  the body of another function too: an exit stays visible where a function
  decides it, decided on 12 October 2026, and the cases of the self-tests
  under bin/ carry one record each for it. A line that defines a function is
  no call of that function; where it calls another such function on the same
  line, as within() { ... || fail ...; } in bin/devloop-bounded does, it
  counts. A name that does not stand at the place of a command, inside a
  string say, as selftest in gone "devloop-bounded-selftest-$$" ..., is no
  call. The body of a function runs from its definition to the brace that
  closes it, braces inside quotes left out. Until 12 October 2026 such a line
  stood in no set; || pass in hooks/pre-tool-use-mark-guard.sh was the case
  the entry of 9 October 2026 on step 8 of setup-checks recorded.
- Under the two check headings in docs/skill-conventions.md ("Before you
  change anything, run this" and "Before a handover, run these"): every
  command block, as one item covered by an anchor on any of its lines.
- In docs/roadmap.md under "Known gaps": no line, since 2 November 2026,
  when the defects left the roadmap for docs/defects.md and the roadmap
  became a report the tool reads for the heads a block's Found names, for
  the lines a run's record points at and for the proofs of closings. Since
  4 November 2026 the dated report is docs/roadmap.md and its archives
  docs/roadmap-archive-<n>.md, section F: the entries up to that day stand
  in docs/roadmap-archive-1.md, byte for byte the roadmap of d026802, and
  the tool reads an archive for the same three things and for nothing else;
  no line of an archive is in the set, and its table under "Named, not
  built as skills" is read nowhere, neither for the names of the note form
  nor for the set - the item below reads docs/roadmap.md alone. Until
  then every entry head (a line starting with "- **") and every line holding
  "Recorded, not built", "Built", "Half built" or "Should hold", or the word
  "Closed", the word of a closing line under D since 20 October 2026, stood
  in the set, each covered by the defect it named or by a not-a-thing record;
  and from 12 October 2026 one of those status words with a line break
  between two of its words was listed under a heading of its own and turned
  the tool red, since it stood in no line of the set, a check that went with
  the set.
- In docs/roadmap.md under "Named, not built as skills": every row of the
  table except the header row and the separator row. Those rows are the only
  record of the names the stock-take accounts for, each as a thing of kind
  named skill or as "part of" the thing that does its work, so a name added
  to the table is seen as uncovered until an order takes it.
  The table under "Built and exercised" stays out: what it claims about the
  twelve skills, the things of those skills carry themselves.

A line is covered when an anchor of any record that is not rejected (thing,
run or not-a-thing) or of any block that is not rejected (its Fix, Trigger,
Bench or Cost) lies on it. A line of the set that can
carry no unique anchor at all (too short and repeated, or repeated whole)
leaves the set and is listed under a heading of its own, so it is seen rather
than silently dropped; its branch is anchored on the nearest line that can
carry an anchor, normally the condition itself. The exit lines of a hook are
the case: pass and block are anchored where they are decided.

Every order from 2 to 9 of the stock-take ends when the tool reports, for the
files that order took, no uncovered line of the set, no unit without a
straight path and no broken record.

F. THE DEFECTS, docs/defects.md

Every defect of the set, open or closed, is one block of docs/defects.md,
since 2 November 2026, decided by the person on that day, the roadmap entry
of that date on the defects file: a heading "### <title>" and one field per
physical line under it, "- Wrong: ", "- Should: ", "- Found: ", "- Fix: "
and "- Status: " in every block, "- Note: ", "- Trigger: ", "- Bench: " and
"- Cost: " where there is something to say. The file opens with an
introduction, every line before the first heading of the second level, which
the tool does not read; then the sections, "## Milestone <number>" for every
milestone under "## Twelve milestones, in this order" in docs/plan.md that
takes defects, read off its headings "### <number>. ", and "## Without a
milestone"; and, as a mark of the transition, "## Not yet sorted", which
holds every block no order has sorted into a milestone yet. A closed defect
stays under its milestone.

The fields. The title is unique in the file, carries no " / " - the names
of things are made of it, and a run names a thing by its name or a defect
by its title, the two never alike - and does not change with the section.
Wrong and Should carry the text; "not stated", the second mark of the
transition, stands where no text of the roadmap filled them when the blocks
were written. Found carries the head of the entry of docs/roadmap.md, or of
an archive docs/roadmap-archive-<n>.md of it, that found the defect, whole -
the text between "- **" and its closing "**", read across line breaks with
its whitespace collapsed - and naming exactly one head under "Known gaps"
over the roadmap and its archives together: a head standing in two of these
files is not exactly one, and the message names the files. Fix carries the
place of the repair as `FILE` "TEXT", an anchor by the rules of section C,
found exactly once and never in docs/roadmap.md, an archive of it or
docs/defects.md, where no repair stands; or "none", where
nothing is built; or "not determined", where nobody has read whether anything
is. Status carries the state in the words of section D, or the closing in
one of its three forms. Trigger, Bench and Cost carry anchors, the roadmap
allowed. Note is free text.

The tool rejects, as a broken record with the line of docs/defects.md: a
block missing a required field; a title standing twice, or carrying " / ",
or being a thing's name; a field over a line break, or a line that is no
field, or a field twice; a Fix in none of its forms, pointing at
docs/roadmap.md, an archive of it or docs/defects.md, not found exactly
once, or under a directory section D classifies nowhere; a Trigger, Bench or Cost that is no
anchor; a Found naming no head whole and exactly once; a section "##
Milestone <number>" whose number the plan does not carry, or a heading of
the second level in none of the three forms; a Status in none of the forms;
a closing whose proof does not hold; a non-empty line that belongs to no
introduction, heading or block; and a block whose Status names another
state than the tool computes. A run on a rejected block is rejected with
it. The blocks under "## Not yet sorted" and the blocks with "not stated"
are listed, each under a heading of its own, and turn nothing red. The
output shows the defects by section, the states counted per section.

No block vanishes in silence, since 2 November 2026, decided by the person
on that day, the roadmap entry of that date on the guard over the blocks.
The tool holds the blocks of docs/defects.md at the commit this branch
forked from, git merge-base HEAD origin/main, against the working tree. A
title standing there and not here is a block removed or renamed; a title
standing in both with another Should is a Should changed; and a renamed
block whose new title carries in the working tree another Should than its
old title carried at the branch point is a Should changed as well, named
under its new title, since 2 November 2026, the addendum of that day to the
entry on the guard, before which the line of the renaming alone passed such
a block. Each such change is named by one physical line the branch adds to
docs/roadmap.md, read off git diff of that file against the branch point, in
one of three fixed forms, the title written whole after the prefix to the
end of the line, whatever it carries - quotes, backticks, a colon -, the two
titles of a renaming separated by " / ", which no title carries:

    Block removed: TITLE
    Block renamed: OLD TITLE / NEW TITLE
    Should changed: TITLE

None of them opens with "- **", the opening of an entry's head, which the
step of the workflow on titles and the reading of Found take for a head. The
tool is red where a change has no such line, and where a line names what the
branch did not do: a title that stands in the working tree still or stood at
the branch point in no block, a new title that stands nowhere or stood there
already, a Should that is the branch point's, under the same title or, for a
renamed block, under its old title. A block the branch adds needs no line.
Without origin/main, or without a commit HEAD and origin/main share, the
tool says in one line that the blocks are not held against a branch point,
and is not red for it; where docs/defects.md does not stand at the branch
point, that there is nothing to compare. On main after a squash merge HEAD
is the branch point, and nothing differs. A branch behind main, whose main
gained a block after the branch point, is held against the branch point and
not against main's tip, so that block is no removal.

An archive of the roadmap never changes, since 4 November 2026, decided by
the person on that day, the roadmap entry of that date on the archive. The
roadmap is the dated report in docs/roadmap.md and its archives
docs/roadmap-archive-<n>.md; an archive is docs/roadmap.md as it stood at
the branch point of the order that archived it, byte for byte, every line
at its number, the four undated sections included, and a later order
archives the roadmap again under the next number: docs/roadmap-archive-1.md
is the roadmap of d026802, the entries up to the order of 4 November 2026.
The tool reads an archive for three things and nothing else: the heads a
Found names, the lines a run's record, a Trigger, a Bench, a Cost or a
closing points at, and the proofs of closings, the closing lines that quote
the text left out of the count as for docs/roadmap.md. A Fix points at no
archive, as at no roadmap, since no repair stands there; the table under
"Named, not built as skills" of an archive is read nowhere; and no line of
an archive is in a search set. The tool holds the archives against git
merge-base HEAD origin/main as it holds the blocks: an archive that stood at
the branch point and is changed, renamed or removed in the working tree is
red with its name; an archive the branch adds is red unless it is
docs/roadmap.md as it stood at the branch point byte for byte - compared as
git's blob of each, read with git hash-object, so that a checkout which
converts line endings is no change - and carries the next free number, the
highest number at the branch point plus one, so that a second new archive
on one branch is red as well; where no docs/roadmap.md stood at the branch
point, nothing could be archived, and a new archive is red. Without
origin/main, or without a commit HEAD and origin/main share, the tool says
in one line that the archives are not held against a branch point, and is
not red for it; on main after a squash merge HEAD is the branch point and
nothing differs. The report shows the result under a heading of its own. A
wrong line number in an archived entry is not corrected there: the entry of
the branch that finds it says what is right, and the correction in place
that docs/skill-conventions.md allows a dated entry holds for docs/roadmap.md
alone.

LIMITS

- It proves that an anchor stands where a record says, not that it supports
  the record; and that a Found names a head, not that the entry under that
  head is the one that found the defect.
- A closing's proof shows that a text is absent from a file and once stood
  in it, that a sentence stands in a file once, or that a line stands; it
  does not show that the text was the defect's place, that the corrected
  sentence is right, or that the line decides what the record says it does.
- A condition phrased without any of the search words is caught only by the
  headings, items, list heads and table rows, not with certainty.
- The version rule sees the lines a thing stands on; a line of a branch that
  no record names goes unseen.
- Walked, for shared text, means walked in at least one skill that inserts it.
- A version read from a session log is recorded with its source; nobody else
  can read that log again. Two of the session logs of the benches carry no
  version at all; that cannot be repaired.
- Text that moved unchanged into shared/ on 18 September 2026 counts for no
  run before it moved, although those runs received the same words.
- Coverage counts lines, not branches. A line carrying two conditions is
  covered by the first record on it.
- A defect whose repair stands on disk while its block's Fix says "none"
  reads "Recorded, not built"; the tool cannot see the repair. Only a named
  skill whose directory exists is flagged.
- The tool checks that a name's source and unit match the site, not that the
  outcome part describes the outcome, and not that a "per" names the right
  thing.
- The count of messages at the end of the self-test is read off the places
  the tool emits one - an append to a record's errors, to the broken or the
  table-error list, an assignment to a run's reason or info or to the refusal,
  a print to stderr, a RuntimeError raised - and each template is matched
  against the messages the self-test's analyses produced and the text its
  cases asserted. A message emitted another way is not counted, and neither
  is any line of the report, what render() prints: a report line is walked
  only where a case reads the rendered report, which three cases do - a
  table with no root, a refused repository, a table where every run counts.
  So "73 of 73" says nothing about the report. What the self-test never
  reaches is measured with python3 -m trace --count --missing over it, with
  --coverdir pointing outside the repository, since the check over untracked
  files under scripts/ reports what it leaves behind; on 23 September 2026
  that listed the three report lines above, since cased, and beside them
  only lines of the self-test itself that a passing run cannot reach. A
  message listed as asserted by no case is printed, not red.
- Copy detection is staged with the extra file changed in the same commit,
  because git blame -C -C looks only at the files that commit changed: a
  copy from a file the commit left alone goes unseen, by git and so by the
  information line.
- The tool checks that a reach claim agrees with the computed set, not that
  the call stands where the claim says: which side of a skill's boundary a
  call stands on is read by whoever writes the record. A call in shared text
  counts for every skill that inserts the file wherever the insert stands, so
  a call inserted onto a path the mode does not take is derived as reached all
  the same.
- Whether a shell function ends the program is read off the word exit on the
  lines of its body and off calls of such functions; a program that ends
  another way - a trap, exec, set -e, a kill of its own process - is not
  seen, and a function whose closing brace is not found is read to the end
  of its file.
- The squash check reads what stands when the tool runs: origin/main as last
  fetched, and the version the working tree carries. A version raised after
  the tool last ran on the branch is seen only by the next run, on the branch
  or on main after the merge; the guard in scripts/devloop-version-guard sees
  it at the edit.
- The check against a run recorded too early reads the commit of the run's
  row off git blame of the table, and only where the tree's version was
  introduced on this branch; section C says what it does not see - a row
  re-anchored after the change, a run recorded in the one commit with the
  change, and main after the squash - and why the run's date is not read.
- The blocks are held against the branch point by title and Should alone,
  and against origin/main as last fetched: a block removed and written
  again with the same title and Should is no change, a Should changed and
  changed back none, a block that moved between sections or lost another
  field none; a renaming named as a removal and an addition passes where a
  line names the removal; and a line of the fixed form that the branch
  added to the roadmap and took out again is not read, since the diff
  against the branch point does not carry it.
- The archives are held against origin/main as last fetched, by git's blob
  of each file, and by name: an archive renamed is a removal and an addition,
  both red; an archive at the branch point that a branch changes and changes
  back is no change; and that an archive's text is the roadmap of the day it
  names, beyond the branch that added it, is read by nobody after the merge.
"""

import ast
import contextlib
import io
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile

COLUMNS = [
    "type", "name", "kind", "file", "anchor", "evidence_file",
    "evidence_anchor", "note", "date", "version", "commit", "source",
    "run_file", "run_anchor", "trigger_file", "trigger_anchor", "bench_file",
    "bench_anchor", "cost_file", "cost_anchor",
]
TYPES = ("thing", "run", "not-a-thing")
KINDS = (
    "skill branch", "shared text", "output style", "hook outcome",
    "hook registration", "program outcome", "check command", "named skill",
)
DEFECT = "defect"
SOURCES = ("entry", "log", "none")
STATES = ("Undetermined", "Recorded, not built", "Built, never walked", "Walked",
          "Closed")
SHIPPED = ("skills", "shared", "output-styles", "hooks", "bin", "scripts",
           ".github")
RECORDED_IN = ("docs", "README.md")
ZERO_HASH = "0" * 40
REASONS = ("part of", "rationale", "quoted", "template")
NOT_STATED = "outcome with nobody there not stated"
ROOT = "enters the unattended mode"
NOBODY_NONE = "with nobody there: the mode does not reach this skill"
STRAIGHT = "straight path"
WHOLE_FILE = "whole file"
NAMED_HEADING = "## Named, not built as skills"
KNOWN_GAPS = "## Known gaps"
DEFECTS = "docs/defects.md"
ROADMAP = "docs/roadmap.md"
# an archive of the roadmap, docs/roadmap.md as it stood at the branch point
# of the order that archived it, byte for byte, never changed: read for the
# heads a Found names, the lines a record points at and the proofs of
# closings, and for nothing else (section F)
ARCHIVE = re.compile(r"^docs/roadmap-archive-(\d+)\.md$")
PLAN = "docs/plan.md"
MILESTONES_HEADING = "## Twelve milestones, in this order"
BLOCK_FIELDS = ("Wrong", "Should", "Found", "Fix", "Status", "Note", "Trigger",
                "Bench", "Cost")
REQUIRED_FIELDS = ("Wrong", "Should", "Found", "Fix", "Status")
FIX_NONE = "none"
FIX_UNDETERMINED = "not determined"
NOT_STATED_MARK = "not stated"
NOT_YET_SORTED = "## Not yet sorted"
WITHOUT_MILESTONE = "## Without a milestone"
# the fixed forms of section F, one physical line each, read off the lines a
# branch adds to docs/roadmap.md; the title stands whole after the prefix
BLOCK_REMOVED = "Block removed: "
BLOCK_RENAMED = "Block renamed: "
SHOULD_CHANGED = "Should changed: "
RENAME_SEP = " / "

PROSE_WORDS = re.compile(
    r"\b(if|where|when|whenever|wherever|unless|otherwise|either|else|before)\b",
    re.IGNORECASE)
SHELL_WORDS = re.compile(r"\b(if|elif|else|case|exit)\b")
PYTHON_WORDS = re.compile(r"\b(if|elif|raise|exit|except)\b")
WORKFLOW_WORDS = re.compile(
    r"\b(if|elif|else|case|exit)\b|\b(run|branches|timeout-minutes|cancel-in-progress):")
COMMENT = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*#")
FUNCTION_DEF = re.compile(
    r"^\s*(?:function\s+)?([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\s*\(\s*\)"
    r"|^\s*function\s+([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\b")
EXIT_WORD = re.compile(r"\bexit\b")
HEADING = re.compile(r"^#{1,6}\s")
NUMBERED = re.compile(r"^\s*\d+\.\s")
BOLD_LIST_HEAD = re.compile(r"^\s*[-*]\s+\*\*")
BOLD_PARA_HEAD = re.compile(r"^\*\*")
TABLE_ROW = re.compile(r"^\s*\|")
TABLE_SEPARATOR = re.compile(r"^\s*\|?\s*:?-{3,}")
ENTRY_HEAD = re.compile(r"^- \*\*")
CLOSED_WORD = re.compile(r"\bClosed\b")
CLOSED_FORMS = (
    ("lapsed", re.compile(
        r'Closed, lapsed: `([^`]+)` no longer carries "([^"]+)"'
        r'(?:, removed at `([0-9a-f]{7,40})`)?\.')),
    ("corrected in the document", re.compile(
        r'Closed, corrected in the document: `([^`]+)` carries "([^"]+)"\.')),
    ("left on purpose", re.compile(
        r'Closed, left on purpose: decided in `([^`]+)` at "([^"]+)"\.')),
)
DATE = re.compile(r"^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$")
FENCE = re.compile(r"^ {0,3}(```|~~~)")
INSERT = re.compile(r"^!`\$\{CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT\}/bin/devloop-text ([a-z0-9-]+)`$")
CALLS = re.compile(r"^calls ([a-z0-9-]+)$")
NOBODY = re.compile(r"^with nobody there: (reached|not reached|not stated) per (.+)$")
DOES = re.compile(r"^does the work of ([a-z0-9-]+)$")
NAMED_ROW = re.compile(r"^\|\s*`([a-z0-9-]+)`\s*\|")
MILESTONE_SECTION = re.compile(r"^## Milestone (\d+)$")
PLAN_MILESTONE = re.compile(r"^### (\d+)\. ")
BLOCK_HEAD = re.compile(r"^### (.*)$")
FIELD_LINE = re.compile(r"^- ([A-Z][a-z]+): (.*)$")
ANCHOR_FIELD = re.compile(r'^`([^`]+)` "(.+)"$')
CHECK_HEADINGS = (
    "## Before you change anything, run this",
    "## Before a handover, run these",
)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- helpers

class Refused(Exception):
    """The repository is one the tool refuses to work on."""


def git(root, *args, check=True):
    p = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root] + list(args), capture_output=True,
                       text=True)
    if check and p.returncode != 0:
        raise RuntimeError("git %s: %s" % (" ".join(args), p.stderr.strip()))
    return p.stdout


def git_ok(root, *args):
    return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root] + list(args),
                          capture_output=True, text=True).returncode == 0


def resolve(root, ref):
    p = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify",
                        ref + "^{commit}"], capture_output=True, text=True)
    return p.stdout.strip() if p.returncode == 0 else None


def blob_at(root, commit, rel):
    """The id of the blob the file has at the commit, or None where the
    file does not stand there."""
    return git(root, "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", "%s:%s" % (commit, rel),
               check=False).strip() or None


def blob_of(root, rel):
    """The id git would store for the file as it stands in the working
    tree, its clean filter applied, so that a checkout which converts line
    endings is no change; None where the file cannot be read."""
    return git(root, "hash-object", "--", rel, check=False).strip() or None


def is_archive(rel):
    return ARCHIVE.match(rel) is not None


def archive_files(root):
    """The archives of the roadmap in the working tree, (number, path) by
    number."""
    out = []
    try:
        names = os.listdir(os.path.join(root, "docs"))
    except OSError:
        return out
    for fn in names:
        m = ARCHIVE.match("docs/" + fn)
        if m and os.path.isfile(os.path.join(root, "docs", fn)):
            out.append((int(m.group(1)), "docs/" + fn))
    return sorted(out)


class Git:
    """Ancestry and dates, each asked once."""

    def __init__(self, root):
        self.root = root
        self.ancestry = {}
        self.dates = {}
        self.blames = {}

    def is_ancestor(self, a, b):
        """True when commit a is an ancestor of commit b, or the same commit."""
        key = (a, b)
        if key not in self.ancestry:
            self.ancestry[key] = git_ok(self.root, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor",
                                        a, b)
        return self.ancestry[key]

    def commit_date(self, h):
        """The committer date, in the commit's own timezone, as YYYY-MM-DD."""
        if h not in self.dates:
            self.dates[h] = git(self.root, "show", "-s", "--format=%ci",
                                h).strip()[:10]
        return self.dates[h]

    def blame_file(self, rel):
        """lineno -> (hash or None where uncommitted, committer time)."""
        if rel not in self.blames:
            out = {}
            p = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", self.root, "blame",
                                "--line-porcelain", "--", rel],
                               capture_output=True, text=True)
            if p.returncode == 0:
                cur = None
                for line in p.stdout.splitlines():
                    m = re.match(r"^([0-9a-f]{40}) \d+ (\d+)", line)
                    if m:
                        cur = {"hash": m.group(1), "line": int(m.group(2)),
                               "time": 0}
                    elif cur is None:
                        continue
                    elif line.startswith("committer-time "):
                        cur["time"] = int(line.split()[1])
                    elif line.startswith("\t"):
                        h = None if cur["hash"] == ZERO_HASH else cur["hash"]
                        out[cur["line"]] = (h, cur["time"])
                        cur = None
            self.blames[rel] = out
        return self.blames[rel]


def blame_copy(root, path, lineno):
    """What git blame -C -C attributes the line to: (hash, filename) or None."""
    p = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "blame", "--line-porcelain", "-C",
                        "-C", "-L", "%d,%d" % (lineno, lineno), "--", path],
                       capture_output=True, text=True)
    if p.returncode != 0:
        return None
    out = p.stdout.splitlines()
    if not out:
        return None
    h = out[0].split()[0]
    if h == ZERO_HASH:
        return None
    filename = path
    for line in out[1:]:
        if line.startswith("filename "):
            filename = line[len("filename "):]
    return h, filename


def version_commit(root, version):
    """The commit that introduced the version into plugin.json, or None."""
    needle = '"version": "%s"' % version
    out = git(root, "log", "--format=%H", "-S" + needle, "--",
              ".claude-plugin/plugin.json", check=False)
    hashes = out.split()
    for h in reversed(hashes):
        shown = git(root, "show", "%s:.claude-plugin/plugin.json" % h,
                    check=False)
        if needle in shown:
            return h
    return None


def tree_version(root):
    """The version .claude-plugin/plugin.json carries in the working tree, or
    None where the file is not there or names none."""
    text = ""
    with contextlib.suppress(OSError), \
            open(os.path.join(root, ".claude-plugin", "plugin.json"),
                 encoding="utf-8") as f:
        text = f.read()
    m = re.search(r'"version":\s*"([^"]*)"', text)
    return m and m.group(1)


def is_shallow(root):
    return git(root, "rev-parse", "--is-shallow-repository").strip() == "true"


def units_of(lines):
    """(units, fenced line numbers). A unit: heading text or None, hline,
    start, end. The first unit starts at line 1; a file without a heading is
    one unit; a heading inside a fenced code block is not a heading."""
    fenced = set()
    inside = False
    for i, l in enumerate(lines, 1):
        if FENCE.match(l):
            inside = not inside
            fenced.add(i)
        elif inside:
            fenced.add(i)
    heads = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines, 1)
             if HEADING.match(l) and i not in fenced]
    if not heads:
        return [{"heading": None, "part": WHOLE_FILE, "hline": None,
                 "start": 1, "end": len(lines)}], fenced
    units = []
    for k, h in enumerate(heads):
        line = lines[h - 1]
        units.append({
            "heading": line.lstrip("#").strip(),
            "level": len(line) - len(line.lstrip("#")),
            "hline": h,
            "start": 1 if k == 0 else h,
            "end": heads[k + 1] - 1 if k + 1 < len(heads) else len(lines),
        })
    # the chain of enclosing headings, top-most first, own heading last
    for k, u in enumerate(units):
        chain, level = [u["heading"]], u["level"]
        for prev in reversed(units[:k]):
            if prev["level"] < level:
                chain.insert(0, prev["heading"])
                level = prev["level"]
        u["chain"] = chain
    # the unit part of a name: the heading where it is unique in the file,
    # else the shortest tail of the chain no other unit's chain ends with;
    # where even the whole chain is shared, the whole chain, and the two units
    # cannot be told apart
    for u in units:
        tail = u["chain"]
        for n in range(1, len(u["chain"]) + 1):
            if not any(o is not u and o["chain"][-n:] == u["chain"][-n:]
                       for o in units):
                tail = u["chain"][-n:]
                break
        u["part"] = " > ".join(tail)
    return units, fenced


UNIT_TOPS = ("skills", "shared", "output-styles")


def is_unit_file(rel):
    return any(rel.startswith(t + "/") for t in UNIT_TOPS) \
        and rel.endswith(".md")


class Files:
    """Every file read once."""

    def __init__(self, root):
        self.root = root
        self.cache = {}
        self.unit_cache = {}

    def get(self, rel):
        if rel not in self.cache:
            path = os.path.join(self.root, rel)
            try:
                with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
                    text = f.read()
            except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError):
                self.cache[rel] = None
            else:
                self.cache[rel] = (text, text.splitlines())
        return self.cache[rel]

    def units(self, rel):
        if rel not in self.unit_cache:
            got = self.get(rel)
            self.unit_cache[rel] = units_of(got[1]) if got else ([], set())
        return self.unit_cache[rel]

    def unit_at(self, rel, lineno):
        for u in self.units(rel)[0]:
            if u["start"] <= lineno <= u["end"]:
                return u
        return None


def find_anchor(files, rel, anchor, skip=()):
    """(status, lineno). status: ok, missing, multiple, no-file, too-long,
    too-short, whitespace. For multiple, the lines it stands on in place of
    the line. The lines numbered in skip are left out of the count: the
    closing lines of docs/roadmap.md, of its archives and of
    docs/defects.md, which quote the text they name."""
    if anchor != anchor.strip():
        return "whitespace", None
    if len(anchor) > 80:
        return "too-long", None
    got = files.get(rel)
    if got is None:
        return "no-file", None
    text, lines = got
    hits = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines, 1) if anchor in l]
    hits = [i for i in hits if i not in skip]
    total = sum(lines[i - 1].count(anchor) for i in hits)
    if total == 0:
        return "missing", None
    if total > 1:
        return "multiple", hits
    lineno = hits[0]
    if len(anchor) < 20 and lines[lineno - 1].strip() != anchor:
        return "too-short", None
    return "ok", lineno


def anchorable(text, line):
    """Whether some substring of the line could serve as an anchor."""
    s = line.strip()
    if not s:
        return False
    if len(s) < 20:
        return text.count(s) == 1
    length = min(80, len(s))
    return any(text.count(s[i:i + length]) == 1
               for i in range(len(s) - length + 1))


def absolute_path(field):
    return field.startswith("/") or "/Users/" in field or "/home/" in field


def closing_of(line):
    """(reason, file, text, commit or None) where the line carries one of
    the three closing forms of section D, else None."""
    for reason, rx in CLOSED_FORMS:
        m = rx.search(line)
        if m:
            g = m.groups()
            return reason, g[0], g[1], (g[2] if len(g) > 2 else None)
    return None


def closing_lines(files, rel, text):
    """The lines of docs/roadmap.md, of an archive of it or of
    docs/defects.md that carry a closing form naming this text: they quote
    it, and a count over that file leaves them out."""
    got = files.get(rel)
    if got is None:
        return set()
    out = set()
    for i, l in enumerate(got[1], 1):
        c = closing_of(l)
        if c is not None and c[2] == text:
            out.add(i)
    return out


def check_closing(root, files, rec, closing):
    """The proof of a closing, read with the tool's own means for anchors
    and with git; every failure goes to the record's errors. Over
    docs/roadmap.md, its archives and docs/defects.md the closing lines
    that quote the text are left out of the count, since the entry that
    closed the defect carries the proof beside the decision it names, and
    the block carries it in its Status."""
    reason, rel, text, commit = closing
    inside = not rel.startswith("/") and ".." not in rel.split("/")
    found, at = find_anchor(files, rel, text,
                            closing_lines(files, rel, text)
                            if rel in (ROADMAP, DEFECTS) or is_archive(rel)
                            else ()) \
        if inside else ("no-file", None)
    if reason == "lapsed":
        if len(text) < 20:
            rec.errors.append("closed as lapsed: %r is shorter than 20 "
                              "characters and shows no place" % text)
            return
        if found in ("ok", "multiple"):
            rec.errors.append("closed as lapsed: %r stands in %s still, on %s"
                              % (text, rel, lines_named(
                                  at if found == "multiple" else [at])))
            return
        if found != "missing":
            rec.errors.append("closed as lapsed: %r in %s: %s" % (text, rel, {
                "no-file": "the file cannot be " "read",
                "too-long": "longer than 80 " "characters",
                "whitespace": "carries leading or trailing " "whitespace",
            }[found]))
            return
        stood = git(root, "log", "--format=%H", "-S" + text, "--", rel,
                    check=False).split()
        if not stood:
            rec.errors.append("closed as lapsed: %r never stood in %s on "
                              "this history" % (text, rel))
            return
        if commit is None:
            return
        removed = resolve(root, commit)
        why = None
        if removed is None:
            why = "unresolved"
        elif text in git(root, "show", "%s:%s" % (removed, rel), check=False):
            why = "still"
        elif text not in git(root, "show", "%s^:%s" % (removed, rel),
                             check=False):
            why = "parent"
        if why is not None:
            rec.errors.append("closed as lapsed names %s as the commit that "
                              "removed %r from %s, but %s"
                              % (commit, text, rel, {
                                  "unresolved": "it does not resolve here",
                                  "still": "the file carries the text at "
                                           "that commit",
                                  "parent": "the file did not carry the text "
                                            "before it",
                              }[why]))
        return
    if found == "multiple":
        rec.errors.append("closed as %s: %r in %s: found more than once, on "
                          "%s, so it is not an anchor"
                          % (reason, text, rel, lines_named(at)))
    elif found != "ok":
        rec.errors.append("closed as %s: %r in %s: %s" % (reason, text, rel, {
            "missing": "not " "found",
            "no-file": "the file cannot be " "read",
            "too-long": "longer than 80 " "characters",
            "too-short": "shorter than 20 characters and not a whole " "line",
            "whitespace": "carries leading or trailing " "whitespace",
        }[found]))


def head_files(root):
    """docs/roadmap.md and its archives, the files the heads are read in."""
    return [ROADMAP] + [rel for _, rel in archive_files(root)]


def roadmap_heads(files, root):
    """head text -> {file: how often it stands there}, for every entry head
    under "Known gaps" in docs/roadmap.md and in every archive of it, a line
    starting with "- **" read to its closing "**" across line breaks, its
    whitespace collapsed to single spaces."""
    out = {}
    for rel in head_files(root):
        got = files.get(rel)
        if got is None:
            continue
        lines = got[1]
        inside = False
        for i, l in enumerate(lines, 1):
            if l.startswith("## "):
                inside = l.rstrip() == KNOWN_GAPS
                continue
            if inside and ENTRY_HEAD.match(l):
                text = l[4:]
                j = i
                while "**" not in text and j < len(lines):
                    text += " " + lines[j]
                    j += 1
                head = " ".join(text.split("**", 1)[0].split())
                per = out.setdefault(head, {})
                per[rel] = per.get(rel, 0) + 1
    return out


def plan_milestones(files):
    """The numbers of the milestones under "Twelve milestones, in this
    order" in docs/plan.md, read off its headings "### <number>. "."""
    got = files.get(PLAN)
    if got is None:
        return set()
    out = set()
    inside = False
    for l in got[1]:
        if l.startswith("## "):
            inside = l.rstrip() == MILESTONES_HEADING
            continue
        m = PLAN_MILESTONE.match(l) if inside else None
        if m:
            out.add(m.group(1))
    return out


def named_skills(files):
    """The names in the table under "Named, not built as skills"."""
    got = files.get("docs/roadmap.md")
    if got is None:
        return set()
    names = set()
    inside = False
    for l in got[1]:
        if l.startswith("## "):
            inside = l.rstrip() == NAMED_HEADING
            continue
        if inside:
            m = NAMED_ROW.match(l)
            if m:
                names.add(m.group(1))
    return names


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- records

class Record:
    def __init__(self, lineno, fields):
        self.lineno = lineno
        self.f = dict(zip(COLUMNS, fields))
        self.errors = []
        self.anchors = {}   # anchor column -> (status, lineno)
        self.parts = None   # a thing's three name parts
        self.part_of = None  # a not-a-thing's thing

    def __getitem__(self, k):
        return self.f[k]

    def site(self):
        """(file, lineno) where the site anchor resolved, else None."""
        got = self.anchors.get("anchor")
        if got and got[0] == "ok":
            return self.f["file"], got[1]
        return None


def read_table(path):
    """(records, errors). A record with the wrong number of fields is an
    error and is not read."""
    records, errors = [], []
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8", newline="") as f:
            raw = f.read()
    except OSError as e:
        return [], ["cannot read %s: %s" % (path, e)]
    lines = raw.split("\n")
    if lines and lines[-1] == "":
        lines.pop()
    if not lines or lines[0].rstrip("\r").split("\t") != COLUMNS:
        errors.append("line 1: the header is not the %d columns this tool "
                      "names" % len(COLUMNS))
        return [], errors
    for n, line in enumerate(lines[1:], 2):
        line = line.rstrip("\r")
        if line == "":
            continue
        fields = line.split("\t")
        if len(fields) != len(COLUMNS):
            errors.append("line %d: %d fields instead of %d; the record is "
                          "not read" % (n, len(fields), len(COLUMNS)))
            continue
        records.append(Record(n, fields))
    return records, errors


class Block:
    """A defect, one block of docs/defects.md: a heading of the third level
    and one field per line under it."""

    def __init__(self, lineno, title, section):
        self.lineno = lineno
        self.title = title
        self.section = section     # the heading line of its section
        self.fields = {}           # field -> (lineno, value)
        self.errors = []
        self.anchors = {}          # field -> (file, lineno) where resolved
        self.fix = None            # None, FIX_NONE, FIX_UNDETERMINED or (file, lineno)
        self.closing = None        # the confirmed closing, section D

    def value(self, field):
        got = self.fields.get(field)
        return got[1] if got else None


def read_defects(files, rel=DEFECTS):
    """(blocks, sections, errors). Every non-empty line of docs/defects.md
    belongs to the introduction before the first heading of the second
    level, is such a heading, is a block heading, or is a field line of the
    block above it; any other line is an error with its number."""
    blocks, sections, errors = [], [], []
    got = files.get(rel)
    if got is None:
        errors.append((0, "%s cannot be read, and the defects stand nowhere "
                       "else" % rel))
        return blocks, sections, errors
    section, block = None, None
    for i, l in enumerate(got[1], 1):
        if l.startswith("## "):
            section = l.rstrip()
            sections.append((i, section))
            block = None
            if section != NOT_YET_SORTED and section != WITHOUT_MILESTONE \
                    and not MILESTONE_SECTION.match(section):
                errors.append((i, "heading %r is none of '## Milestone "
                               "<number>', %r and %r" % (section,
                                                         WITHOUT_MILESTONE,
                                                         NOT_YET_SORTED)))
            continue
        if section is None:
            continue
        m = BLOCK_HEAD.match(l)
        if m:
            block = Block(i, m.group(1).strip(), section)
            blocks.append(block)
            continue
        if l.strip() == "":
            continue
        m = FIELD_LINE.match(l)
        if block is None:
            errors.append((i, "a line under a section heading that opens no "
                           "block"))
        elif not m or m.group(1) not in BLOCK_FIELDS:
            errors.append((i, "not a field line of the block %r; a field is "
                           "one of %s and stands on one physical line"
                           % (block.title, ", ".join(BLOCK_FIELDS))))
        elif m.group(1) in block.fields:
            errors.append((i, "the block %r carries %s twice"
                           % (block.title, m.group(1))))
        else:
            block.fields[m.group(1)] = (i, m.group(2).strip())
    return blocks, sections, errors


class _Text:
    """A file given as text, read where Files would be read."""

    def __init__(self, text):
        self.text = text

    def get(self, rel):
        return (self.text, self.text.split("\n"))


def hold_blocks_to_branch_point(root, defects_rel, blocks, res):
    """Section F, the guard over the blocks: every block of the defects file
    at git merge-base HEAD origin/main against the working tree, and every
    change named by one line of the fixed forms the branch added to
    docs/roadmap.md. Fills res.guard and res.guard_errors."""
    if resolve(root, "origin/main") is None:
        res.guard = {"note": "origin/main is not here, so the blocks are not "
                             "held against a branch point"}
        return
    p = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "merge-base", "HEAD",
                        "origin/main"], capture_output=True, text=True)
    if p.returncode != 0:
        res.guard = {"note": "HEAD and origin/main share no commit, so the "
                             "blocks are not held against a branch point"}
        return
    base = p.stdout.strip()
    p = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "show",
                        "%s:%s" % (base, defects_rel)],
                       capture_output=True, text=True)
    if p.returncode != 0:
        res.guard = {"base": base,
                     "note": "%s does not stand at %s, the branch point, so "
                             "there is nothing to hold the blocks against"
                             % (defects_rel, base[:7])}
        return
    base_blocks, _, _ = read_defects(_Text(p.stdout), defects_rel)
    at_base, in_tree = {}, {}
    for blk in base_blocks:
        at_base.setdefault(blk.title, blk.value("Should"))
    for blk in blocks:
        in_tree.setdefault(blk.title, blk.value("Should"))
    removed = [t for t in at_base if t not in in_tree]
    added = [t for t in in_tree if t not in at_base]
    changed = [t for t in at_base if t in in_tree and at_base[t] != in_tree[t]]
    diff = git(root, "diff", base, "--", ROADMAP, check=False)
    said_removed, said_renamed, said_changed, n = [], [], [], 0
    for line in diff.split("\n"):
        if not line.startswith("+") or line.startswith("+++"):
            continue
        s = line[1:].strip()
        if s.startswith(BLOCK_REMOVED):
            said_removed.append(s[len(BLOCK_REMOVED):].strip())
        elif s.startswith(BLOCK_RENAMED):
            rest = s[len(BLOCK_RENAMED):]
            if RENAME_SEP not in rest:
                res.guard_errors.append("a line added to %s says '%s%s' "
                                        "without ' / ' between the old title "
                                        "and the new"
                                        % (ROADMAP, BLOCK_RENAMED, rest.strip()))
                continue
            old, new = rest.split(RENAME_SEP, 1)
            said_renamed.append((old.strip(), new.strip()))
        elif s.startswith(SHOULD_CHANGED):
            said_changed.append(s[len(SHOULD_CHANGED):].strip())
        else:
            continue
        n += 1
    accounted = set(said_removed) | {old for old, _ in said_renamed}
    for t in removed:
        if t not in accounted:
            res.guard_errors.append("the block %r stood in %s at %s, the "
                                    "branch point, and stands in the working "
                                    "tree no more, and no line added to %s "
                                    "since says '%s<title>' or '%s<title> / "
                                    "<new title>'" % (t, defects_rel, base[:7],
                                                      ROADMAP, BLOCK_REMOVED,
                                                      BLOCK_RENAMED))
    for t in said_removed:
        if t in in_tree:
            res.guard_errors.append("a line added to %s says '%s%s', and that "
                                    "block stands in the working tree still"
                                    % (ROADMAP, BLOCK_REMOVED, t))
        elif t not in at_base:
            res.guard_errors.append("a line added to %s says '%s%s', and no "
                                    "block of that title stood in %s at %s, "
                                    "the branch point"
                                    % (ROADMAP, BLOCK_REMOVED, t, defects_rel,
                                       base[:7]))
    renamed = []
    for old, new in said_renamed:
        ok = True
        if old in in_tree:
            res.guard_errors.append("a line added to %s says '%s%s / %s', and "
                                    "the block %r stands in the working tree "
                                    "still"
                                    % (ROADMAP, BLOCK_RENAMED, old, new, old))
            ok = False
        elif old not in at_base:
            res.guard_errors.append("a line added to %s says '%s%s / %s', and "
                                    "no block titled %r stood in %s at %s, "
                                    "the branch point"
                                    % (ROADMAP, BLOCK_RENAMED, old, new, old,
                                       defects_rel, base[:7]))
            ok = False
        if new not in in_tree:
            res.guard_errors.append("a line added to %s says '%s%s / %s', and "
                                    "no block titled %r stands in the working "
                                    "tree" % (ROADMAP, BLOCK_RENAMED, old, new,
                                              new))
            ok = False
        elif new in at_base:
            res.guard_errors.append("a line added to %s says '%s%s / %s', and "
                                    "a block titled %r stood in %s at %s, the "
                                    "branch point, already"
                                    % (ROADMAP, BLOCK_RENAMED, old, new, new,
                                       defects_rel, base[:7]))
            ok = False
        if ok:
            renamed.append((old, new))
    for t in changed:
        if t not in said_changed:
            res.guard_errors.append("the Should of the block %r is not the "
                                    "one it carried in %s at %s, the branch "
                                    "point, and no line added to %s since "
                                    "says '%s<title>'"
                                    % (t, defects_rel, base[:7], ROADMAP,
                                       SHOULD_CHANGED))
    # a renaming carries its Should across, since 2 November 2026, the
    # addendum of that day: the Should the old title had at the branch point
    # against the one the new title has in the working tree
    renamed_from = {new: old for old, new in renamed}
    for old, new in renamed:
        if at_base[old] != in_tree[new]:
            changed.append(new)
            if new not in said_changed:
                res.guard_errors.append("the Should of the block %r, renamed "
                                        "from %r, is not the one that block "
                                        "carried in %s at %s, the branch "
                                        "point, and no line added to %s since "
                                        "says '%s<new title>'"
                                        % (new, old, defects_rel, base[:7],
                                           ROADMAP, SHOULD_CHANGED))
    for t in said_changed:
        if t not in in_tree:
            res.guard_errors.append("a line added to %s says '%s%s', and no "
                                    "block of that title stands in the "
                                    "working tree"
                                    % (ROADMAP, SHOULD_CHANGED, t))
        elif t in renamed_from:
            if in_tree[t] == at_base[renamed_from[t]]:
                res.guard_errors.append("a line added to %s says '%s%s', and "
                                        "its Should is the one the block "
                                        "carried at %s, the branch point, "
                                        "under its old title %r"
                                        % (ROADMAP, SHOULD_CHANGED, t,
                                           base[:7], renamed_from[t]))
        elif t not in at_base:
            res.guard_errors.append("a line added to %s says '%s%s', and no "
                                    "block of that title stood in %s at %s, "
                                    "the branch point" % (ROADMAP, SHOULD_CHANGED,
                                                          t, defects_rel,
                                                          base[:7]))
        elif t not in changed:
            res.guard_errors.append("a line added to %s says '%s%s', and its "
                                    "Should is the one it carried at %s, the "
                                    "branch point" % (ROADMAP, SHOULD_CHANGED, t,
                                                      base[:7]))
    old_titles = {old for old, _ in renamed}
    res.guard = {"base": base, "renamed": renamed, "changed": changed,
                 "removed": [t for t in removed if t not in old_titles],
                 "added": [t for t in added if t not in {new for _, new in renamed}],
                 "lines": n}


def hold_archives_to_branch_point(root, res):
    """Section F, the archives of the roadmap against git merge-base HEAD
    origin/main: every archive that stood there is in the working tree as
    it stood, by git's blob, and every archive the branch adds is
    docs/roadmap.md at the branch point, by its blob, under the next free
    number. Fills res.archives and res.archive_errors."""
    origin = resolve(root, "origin/main")
    if origin is None:
        res.archives = {"note": "origin/main is not here, so the archives "
                                "of the roadmap are not held against a "
                                "branch point"}
        return
    # git merge-base prints nothing and ends with 1 where the two share no
    # commit
    base = git(root, "merge-base", "HEAD", "origin/main", check=False).strip()
    if not base:
        res.archives = {"note": "HEAD and origin/main share no commit, so "
                                "the archives of the roadmap are not held "
                                "against a branch point"}
        return
    listed = git(root, "ls-tree", "-r", "--name-only", base, "--", "docs",
                 check=False).split("\n")
    at_base = {rel: int(ARCHIVE.match(rel).group(1)) for rel in listed
               if ARCHIVE.match(rel)}
    held, added = [], []
    for rel in sorted(at_base, key=at_base.get):
        if not os.path.isfile(os.path.join(root, rel)):
            res.archive_errors.append("the archive %s stood at %s, the branch "
                                      "point, and stands in the working tree "
                                      "no more: an archive is neither renamed "
                                      "nor removed" % (rel, base[:7]))
        elif blob_of(root, rel) != blob_at(root, base, rel):
            res.archive_errors.append("the archive %s is not what stood at "
                                      "%s, the branch point: an archive never "
                                      "changes" % (rel, base[:7]))
        else:
            held.append(rel)
    nxt = max(at_base.values(), default=0) + 1
    roadmap_blob = blob_at(root, base, ROADMAP)
    for n, rel in archive_files(root):
        if rel in at_base:
            continue
        before = len(res.archive_errors)
        if n != nxt:
            res.archive_errors.append("the archive %s is new on this branch "
                                      "and carries the number %d, where the "
                                      "next free number after %s, the branch "
                                      "point, is %d" % (rel, n, base[:7], nxt))
        if roadmap_blob is None:
            res.archive_errors.append("the archive %s is new on this branch, "
                                      "and no %s stood at %s, the branch "
                                      "point, for it to be a copy of"
                                      % (rel, ROADMAP, base[:7]))
        elif blob_of(root, rel) != roadmap_blob:
            res.archive_errors.append("the archive %s is new on this branch "
                                      "and is not %s as it stood at %s, the "
                                      "branch point" % (rel, ROADMAP, base[:7]))
        if len(res.archive_errors) == before:
            added.append(rel)
    res.archives = {"base": base, "held": held, "added": added}


PAIRS = (("file", "anchor"), ("evidence_file", "evidence_anchor"),
         ("run_file", "run_anchor"), ("trigger_file", "trigger_anchor"),
         ("bench_file", "bench_anchor"), ("cost_file", "cost_anchor"))

USES = {
    "thing": {"name", "kind", "file", "anchor", "evidence_file",
              "evidence_anchor", "note", "trigger_file", "trigger_anchor",
              "bench_file", "bench_anchor", "cost_file", "cost_anchor"},
    "run": {"name", "note", "date", "version", "commit", "source", "run_file",
            "run_anchor"},
    "not-a-thing": {"file", "anchor", "note"},
}
REQUIRED = {
    "thing": ("name", "kind", "file", "anchor"),
    "run": ("name", "date", "source", "run_file", "run_anchor"),
    "not-a-thing": ("file", "anchor", "note"),
}


def validate(rec):
    f = rec.f
    t = f["type"]
    if t not in TYPES:
        rec.errors.append("unknown type %r" % t)
        return
    for col in COLUMNS:
        if col == "type":
            continue
        if col not in USES[t] and f[col] != "":
            rec.errors.append("%s is filled, which a %s does not use"
                              % (col, t))
    for col in REQUIRED[t]:
        if f[col] == "":
            rec.errors.append("%s is empty" % col)
    for col in COLUMNS:
        if absolute_path(f[col]):
            rec.errors.append("%s carries an absolute local path" % col)
    for a, b in PAIRS:
        if (f[a] == "") != (f[b] == ""):
            rec.errors.append("%s and %s: one is empty and the other not"
                              % (a, b))
    if t == "thing":
        if f["kind"] not in KINDS:
            rec.errors.append("unknown kind %r" % f["kind"])
        if f["kind"] in ("check command", "named skill") and f["note"] == "":
            rec.errors.append("note is empty, which a %s may not leave"
                              % f["kind"])
        parts = f["name"].split(" / ")
        if f["name"] != "" and (len(parts) != 3 or any(
                p == "" or p != p.strip() for p in parts)):
            rec.errors.append("name %r does not have the three parts "
                              "<source> / <unit> / <outcome>" % f["name"])
        elif "; " in f["name"]:
            rec.errors.append("name %r carries '; ', which the note forms use "
                              "as their separator" % f["name"])
        elif f["name"] != "":
            rec.parts = parts
    if t == "run":
        if not DATE.match(f["date"]):
            rec.errors.append("date is not YYYY-MM-DD")
        if f["source"] not in SOURCES:
            rec.errors.append("source is not one of entry, log, none")
        has_v, has_c = f["version"] != "", f["commit"] != ""
        if has_v and has_c:
            rec.errors.append("both version and commit are filled")
        if f["source"] == "none" and (has_v or has_c):
            rec.errors.append("source is none but a version or commit stands")
        if f["source"] in ("entry", "log") and not (has_v or has_c):
            rec.errors.append("source is %s but neither version nor commit "
                              "stands" % f["source"])
        if has_c and f["source"] != "entry":
            rec.errors.append("a commit is a run's version only where the "
                              "entry names it (source entry)")
    if t == "not-a-thing" and f["note"] != "":
        head = f["note"].split("; ")[0]
        if head.startswith("part of: "):
            target = head[len("part of: "):].strip()
            if target == "":
                rec.errors.append("part of: names nothing")
            else:
                rec.part_of = target
        elif head not in REASONS or head == "part of":
            rec.errors.append("reason %r is not on the list: %s"
                              % (head, ", ".join(REASONS)))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- analysis

class Result:
    def __init__(self):
        self.refused = None
        self.table_errors = []      # strings
        self.broken = []            # (record lineno, message)
        self.things = []            # dicts
        self.defects = []           # dicts, one per block of docs/defects.md
        self.defect_errors = []     # (line of docs/defects.md, message)
        self.sections = []          # (line, heading) of docs/defects.md
        self.guard = None           # the blocks held against the branch point, section F
        self.guard_errors = []      # strings: a change without its line, a line without its change
        self.archives = None        # the archives of the roadmap held against the branch point, section F
        self.archive_errors = []    # strings: an archive changed, renamed, removed or wrongly added
        self.runs = []              # dicts
        self.unanchorable = []      # (file, lineno, text)
        self.unanchorable_units = []  # (file, lineno, heading)
        self.no_straight_path = []  # (file, lineno, heading)
        self.uncovered = []         # (file, first, last, text)
        self.set_size = 0
        self.covered_lines = {}     # file -> set of linenos
        self.counts = {}            # (state, kind) -> n
        self.reason_counts = {}     # reason -> n
        self.by_file = {}           # site file -> dict of counts, see tally
        self.roots = []             # names of the things carrying ROOT
        self.reached = None         # skill -> how it was reached; None: not computed

    def broken_count(self):
        return len(self.table_errors) + len(self.broken) \
            + len(self.defect_errors) + len(self.guard_errors) \
            + len(self.archive_errors)


TALLY_KEYS = ("things", "straight paths", "branches", "other kinds", "calls",
              "does the work of", NOT_STATED, "not-a-thing")


def tally(res, rel, key, n=1):
    """One count per site file, so a report copies the output."""
    d = res.by_file.setdefault(rel, dict((k, 0) for k in TALLY_KEYS))
    d[key] += n


def note_forms(note):
    """(calls, does the work of, outcome with nobody there not stated)
    carried by a note that passed validation."""
    segs = note.split("; ") if note != "" else []
    calls = sum(1 for s in segs if CALLS.match(s))
    does = sum(1 for s in segs if DOES.match(s))
    not_stated = sum(1 for s in segs if s == NOT_STATED)
    return calls, does, not_stated


def under_check_heading(files, rel, lineno):
    """True where the line lies under one of the two check headings of
    docs/skill-conventions.md: the nearest "## " line above it is one."""
    if rel != "docs/skill-conventions.md":
        return False
    got = files.get(rel)
    if got is None:
        return False
    lines = got[1]
    for i in range(lineno - 1, 0, -1):
        if lines[i - 1].startswith("## "):
            return lines[i - 1].rstrip() in CHECK_HEADINGS
    return False


def classify_location(files, rel, lineno, kind):
    if kind == "check command":
        return "Built"
    if under_check_heading(files, rel, lineno):
        return "Built"
    top = rel.split("/", 1)[0]
    if top in SHIPPED:
        return "Built"
    if top in RECORDED_IN:
        return "Recorded, not built"
    return None


def wanted_by(only):
    def wanted(rel):
        return not only or any(rel == o or rel.startswith(o.rstrip("/") + "/")
                               for o in only)
    return wanted


def walk(root, top):
    out = []
    for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(os.path.join(root, top)):
        dirnames.sort()
        for fn in sorted(filenames):
            out.append(os.path.relpath(os.path.join(dirpath, fn), root))
    return out


def unit_files(files, root, only=None):
    wanted = wanted_by(only)
    out = []
    for top in UNIT_TOPS:
        for rel in walk(root, top):
            if rel.endswith(".md") and wanted(rel) and files.get(rel):
                out.append(rel)
    return out


def inserting_skills(files, root):
    """shared file -> the skills that insert it, directly or through another
    shared file that they insert."""
    direct = {}
    for rel in unit_files(files, root):
        for l in files.get(rel)[1]:
            m = INSERT.match(l)
            if m:
                direct.setdefault(rel, set()).add("shared/%s.md" % m.group(1))
    out = {}
    for rel, inserted in direct.items():
        if not rel.startswith("skills/"):
            continue
        skill = rel.split("/")[1]
        seen, stack = set(), list(inserted)
        while stack:
            s = stack.pop()
            if s in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(s)
            out.setdefault(s, set()).add(skill)
            stack.extend(direct.get(s, ()))
    return out


def skill_of(rel):
    """The skill a file under skills/ belongs to, else None."""
    parts = rel.split("/")
    return parts[1] if rel.startswith("skills/") and len(parts) > 2 else None


def source_of(rel):
    """The source part of a thing's name at this site: the skill's name where
    the site is that skill's SKILL.md, the path from the repository root
    everywhere else."""
    parts = rel.split("/")
    if rel.startswith("skills/") and len(parts) == 3 and parts[2] == "SKILL.md":
        return parts[1]
    return rel


def unit_part_of(files, rel, lineno):
    """The unit part of a thing's name at this site: "whole file" for a file
    that is not markdown (a hook, hooks.json, a program), the heading of the
    unit the site lies in - or its chain where the heading repeats - for
    every markdown file, and "whole file" for one without a heading."""
    if not rel.endswith(".md"):
        return WHOLE_FILE
    u = files.unit_at(rel, lineno)
    return u["part"] if u else WHOLE_FILE


def lines_named(linenos):
    """'line 5', 'lines 5 and 6', 'lines 2, 5 and 6'."""
    if len(linenos) == 1:
        return "line %d" % linenos[0]
    return "lines %s and %d" % (", ".join(str(n) for n in linenos[:-1]),
                                linenos[-1])


def unquoted(line):
    """The line with the text of its quoted strings blanked and a comment
    behind the code cut off; a $( ... ) inside double quotes is code. A
    name inside a string is not at the place of a command, and a brace
    there opens no body."""
    out, stack, i = [], [], 0
    while i < len(line):
        c = line[i]
        state = stack[-1] if stack else "code"
        if state == "'":
            if c == "'":
                stack.pop()
                out.append(c)
            else:
                out.append(" ")
        elif state == '"':
            if c == "\\" and i + 1 < len(line):
                out.append("  ")
                i += 1
            elif c == '"':
                stack.pop()
                out.append(c)
            elif c == "$" and line[i + 1:i + 2] == "(":
                stack.append("(")
                out.append("$(")
                i += 1
            else:
                out.append(" ")
        else:
            if c == "\\" and i + 1 < len(line):
                out.append(line[i:i + 2])
                i += 1
            elif c in ("'", '"'):
                stack.append(c)
                out.append(c)
            elif c == "#" and (i == 0 or line[i - 1] in " \t;(&|"):
                break
            elif c == "(" and state == "(":
                stack.append("(")
                out.append(c)
            elif c == ")" and state == "(":
                stack.pop()
                out.append(c)
            else:
                out.append(c)
        i += 1
    return "".join(out)


def shell_functions(lines):
    """name -> (first line, last line) of every function a shell file
    defines, the body running from the definition to the brace that closes
    it, braces inside quotes and comments left out; a function whose closing
    brace is not found runs to the end of the file."""
    out = {}
    for i, l in enumerate(lines, 1):
        if COMMENT.match(l):
            continue
        m = FUNCTION_DEF.match(l)
        if not m:
            continue
        depth, end = 0, len(lines)
        for j in range(i, len(lines) + 1):
            if COMMENT.match(lines[j - 1]):
                continue
            code = unquoted(lines[j - 1])
            depth += code.count("{") - code.count("}")
            if depth <= 0 and (j > i or "{" in code):
                end = j
                break
        out[m.group(1) or m.group(2)] = (i, end)
    return out


def call_pattern(name):
    """The name at the place of a command: at the start of the line, or after
    ;, &&, ||, |, !, (, {, the parenthesis that closes a case pattern, then,
    do or else."""
    return re.compile(r"(?:^|[;|!({)]|&&|\bthen\b|\bdo\b|\belse\b)[ \t]*"
                      + re.escape(name) + r"(?![A-Za-z0-9_])")


def calls_in(line, names):
    """Whether the line calls one of the named functions at the place of a
    command. The line that defines a function is no call of that function;
    what follows the definition on the line is read."""
    code = unquoted(line)
    m = FUNCTION_DEF.match(code)
    if m:
        code = code[m.end():]
    return any(call_pattern(n).search(code) for n in names)


def ending_functions(lines):
    """The names of the functions that end the program: a line of the body
    other than a comment line holds the word exit, or calls a function that
    ends the program."""
    funcs = shell_functions(lines)
    ends = set()
    changed = True
    while changed:
        changed = False
        for name, (first, last) in funcs.items():
            if name in ends:
                continue
            for j in range(first, last + 1):
                l = lines[j - 1]
                if COMMENT.match(l):
                    continue
                if EXIT_WORD.search(l) or (ends and calls_in(l, ends)):
                    ends.add(name)
                    changed = True
                    break
    return ends


def python_strings(text):
    """The lines of every string that stands as a statement of its own in a
    python program, a docstring; none where the program does not parse."""
    try:
        tree = ast.parse(text)
    except (SyntaxError, ValueError):
        return set()
    out = set()
    for node in ast.walk(tree):
        if isinstance(node, ast.Expr) and isinstance(node.value, ast.Constant) \
                and isinstance(node.value.value, str):
            out.update(range(node.lineno, node.end_lineno + 1))
    return out


def search_set(files, root, only=None):
    """Items of the search set: (file, first_line, last_line, text)."""
    items = []
    wanted = wanted_by(only)
    for rel in unit_files(files, root, only):
        lines = files.get(rel)[1]
        fenced = files.units(rel)[1]
        for i, l in enumerate(lines, 1):
            if TABLE_ROW.match(l):
                nxt = lines[i] if i < len(lines) else ""
                if TABLE_SEPARATOR.match(l) or TABLE_SEPARATOR.match(nxt):
                    continue
                items.append((rel, i, i, l))
            elif (PROSE_WORDS.search(l)
                  or (HEADING.match(l) and i not in fenced)
                  or NUMBERED.match(l) or BOLD_LIST_HEAD.match(l)
                  or BOLD_PARA_HEAD.match(l)):
                items.append((rel, i, i, l))
    for top in ("hooks", "bin", "scripts", ".github"):
        for rel in walk(root, top):
            if not wanted(rel):
                continue
            if top == ".github" and not rel.startswith(".github/workflows/"):
                continue
            got = files.get(rel)
            if got is None:
                continue
            text, lines = got
            words, strings, ends = SHELL_WORDS, set(), set()
            if top == ".github":
                words = WORKFLOW_WORDS
            elif top != "hooks" and lines and "python" in lines[0]:
                words, strings = PYTHON_WORDS, python_strings(text)
            else:
                ends = ending_functions(lines)
            for i, l in enumerate(lines, 1):
                if COMMENT.match(l) or i in strings:
                    continue
                if words.search(l) or (ends and calls_in(l, ends)):
                    items.append((rel, i, i, l))
    rel = "docs/skill-conventions.md"
    if wanted(rel) and files.get(rel) is not None:
        lines = files.get(rel)[1]
        inside = False
        start = None
        for i, l in enumerate(lines, 1):
            if l.startswith("## "):
                inside = l.rstrip() in CHECK_HEADINGS
            if inside and l.startswith("    "):
                if start is None:
                    start = i
            else:
                if start is not None:
                    items.append((rel, start, i - 1, lines[start - 1]))
                    start = None
        if start is not None:
            items.append((rel, start, len(lines), lines[start - 1]))
    rel = "docs/roadmap.md"
    if wanted(rel) and files.get(rel) is not None:
        lines = files.get(rel)[1]
        inside = None
        for i, l in enumerate(lines, 1):
            if l.startswith("## "):
                inside = l.rstrip()
                continue
            if inside == NAMED_HEADING and TABLE_ROW.match(l):
                nxt = lines[i] if i < len(lines) else ""
                if TABLE_SEPARATOR.match(l) or TABLE_SEPARATOR.match(nxt):
                    continue
                items.append((rel, i, i, l))
    return items


def thing_range(files, rec, part_of_lines):
    """(file, lineno) -> role, for every line the thing stands on."""
    rng = {}
    rel, sline = rec.site()
    if rec.parts[2] == STRAIGHT and is_unit_file(rel):
        u = files.unit_at(rel, sline)
        for n in range(u["start"], u["end"] + 1):
            rng[(rel, n)] = "unit"
        visited = set()

        def follow(frel, first, last):
            lines = files.get(frel)[1]
            for n in range(first, last + 1):
                m = INSERT.match(lines[n - 1])
                if not m:
                    continue
                srel = "shared/%s.md" % m.group(1)
                if srel in visited or files.get(srel) is None:
                    continue
                visited.add(srel)
                slines = files.get(srel)[1]
                for k in range(1, len(slines) + 1):
                    rng.setdefault((srel, k), "inserted")
                follow(srel, 1, len(slines))
        follow(rel, u["start"], u["end"])
    else:
        rng[(rel, sline)] = "site"
    ev = rec.anchors.get("evidence_anchor")
    if ev and ev[0] == "ok":
        rng.setdefault((rec["evidence_file"], ev[1]), "evidence")
    for p in part_of_lines.get(rec["name"], []):
        rng.setdefault(p, "part of")
    return rng


def analyse(root, table=None, only=None, defects=None):
    res = Result()
    if table is None:
        table = os.path.join(root, "docs", "stock-take.tsv")
    table_rel = os.path.relpath(table, root)
    defects_rel = os.path.relpath(defects, root) if defects else DEFECTS
    try:
        if is_shallow(root):
            res.refused = ("shallow clone: git blame reports every line as "
                           "the boundary commit, so nothing here can be "
                           "computed")
            return res
    except RuntimeError as e:
        res.refused = str(e)
        return res
    files = Files(root)
    g = Git(root)
    records, res.table_errors = read_table(table)
    for rec in records:
        validate(rec)
    # anchors
    for rec in records:
        for a, b in PAIRS:
            if rec[a] == "":
                continue
            status, lineno = find_anchor(files, rec[a], rec[b])
            rec.anchors[b] = (status, lineno)
            if status == "multiple":
                rec.errors.append("%s %r in %s: found more than once, on %s, "
                                  "so it is not an anchor"
                                  % (b, rec[b], rec[a], lines_named(lineno)))
            elif status != "ok":
                rec.errors.append("%s %r in %s: %s" % (b, rec[b], rec[a], {
                    "missing": "not found",
                    "no-file": "the file cannot be read",
                    "too-long": "longer than 80 characters",
                    "too-short": "shorter than 20 characters and not a whole line",
                    "whitespace": "carries leading or trailing whitespace",
                }[status]))
    things = [r for r in records if r["type"] == "thing"]
    thing_names = {r["name"] for r in things if r["name"] != ""}
    by_name = {}
    for r in things:
        by_name.setdefault(r["name"], r)
    # names against sites; duplicates; named skills; straight paths per unit
    seen_anchor, seen_name, sp_by_unit = {}, {}, {}
    for rec in things:
        site = rec.site()
        if rec.parts and site:
            src = source_of(site[0])
            if rec.parts[0] != src:
                rec.errors.append("name's source part %r is not %r, where "
                                  "the site stands" % (rec.parts[0], src))
        if rec.parts and site:
            heading = unit_part_of(files, site[0], site[1])
            if rec.parts[1] != heading and heading == WHOLE_FILE:
                rec.errors.append("name's unit part %r is not %r, which a "
                                  "file without units takes"
                                  % (rec.parts[1], WHOLE_FILE))
            elif rec.parts[1] != heading:
                rec.errors.append("name's unit part %r is not the heading of "
                                  "the unit the site lies in, %r"
                                  % (rec.parts[1], heading))
        if rec.parts and site and is_unit_file(site[0]):
            rel, lineno = site
            u = files.unit_at(rel, lineno)
            heading = u["part"]
            if rec.parts[2] == STRAIGHT:
                want = ("skill branch" if rel.startswith("skills/")
                        else "shared text" if rel.startswith("shared/")
                        else "output style")
                if rec["kind"] != want:
                    rec.errors.append("a straight path under %s/ is of kind "
                                      "%s" % (rel.split("/")[0], want))
                key = (rel, u["start"])
                if key in sp_by_unit:
                    rec.errors.append("second straight path of the unit %r "
                                      "in %s (the first on line %d)"
                                      % (heading, rel, sp_by_unit[key]))
                else:
                    sp_by_unit[key] = rec.lineno
        elif rec.parts and rec.parts[2] == STRAIGHT and site:
            rec.errors.append("a straight path stands only in a file under "
                              "skills/, shared/ or output-styles/")
        key = (rec["file"], rec["anchor"])
        if key in seen_anchor:
            rec.errors.append("same site anchor as the thing on line %d"
                              % seen_anchor[key])
        else:
            seen_anchor[key] = rec.lineno
        if rec["name"] in seen_name:
            rec.errors.append("same name as the thing on line %d"
                              % seen_name[rec["name"]])
        else:
            seen_name[rec["name"]] = rec.lineno
        if rec["kind"] == "named skill" and rec.parts and os.path.isdir(
                os.path.join(root, "skills", rec.parts[2])):
            rec.errors.append("named skill, but skills/%s exists"
                              % rec.parts[2])
    # evidence lies where a state can be read off it, or the record is
    # rejected; checked here, before coverage and before the runs, so that a
    # run on such a thing is rejected with it instead of vanishing
    for rec in things:
        ev = rec.anchors.get("evidence_anchor")
        if ev and ev[0] == "ok" and classify_location(
                files, rec["evidence_file"], ev[1], rec["kind"]) is None:
            rec.errors.append("evidence under %s is neither a shipped "
                              "directory nor docs/ or README.md"
                              % rec["evidence_file"])
    # the defects: every block of docs/defects.md, its fields, its anchors,
    # its closing where it carries one, read here before the runs so that a
    # run on a rejected block is rejected with it
    blocks, res.sections, block_errors = read_defects(files, defects_rel)
    res.defect_errors.extend(block_errors)
    heads = roadmap_heads(files, root)
    read_for_heads = head_files(root)
    milestones = plan_milestones(files)
    seen_title = {}
    for blk in blocks:
        if blk.title in seen_title:
            blk.errors.append("the title %r stands on line %d already"
                              % (blk.title, seen_title[blk.title]))
        else:
            seen_title[blk.title] = blk.lineno
        if " / " in blk.title:
            blk.errors.append("the title %r carries ' / ', which the names of "
                              "things are made of" % blk.title)
        if blk.title in thing_names:
            blk.errors.append("the title %r is the name of a thing" % blk.title)
        for field in REQUIRED_FIELDS:
            if field not in blk.fields:
                blk.errors.append("the field %s is missing" % field)
        m = MILESTONE_SECTION.match(blk.section)
        if m and m.group(1) not in milestones:
            blk.errors.append("stands under %r, and %s is no milestone under "
                              "%r in %s" % (blk.section, m.group(1),
                                            MILESTONES_HEADING, PLAN))
        found = blk.value("Found")
        if found is not None:
            per = heads.get(" ".join(found.split()), {})
            n = sum(per.values())
            if n != 1:
                # the files named: every file read where the head stands
                # nowhere, the files it stands in where it stands twice
                blk.errors.append("Found names %s under %r in %s: %r"
                                  % ("no head" if n == 0
                                     else "a head standing %d times" % n,
                                     KNOWN_GAPS,
                                     " or ".join(read_for_heads) if n == 0
                                     else " and ".join(
                                         f for f in read_for_heads if f in per),
                                     found))
        for field in ("Fix", "Trigger", "Bench", "Cost"):
            v = blk.value(field)
            if v is None:
                continue
            if field == "Fix" and v in (FIX_NONE, FIX_UNDETERMINED):
                blk.fix = v
                continue
            m = ANCHOR_FIELD.match(v)
            if not m:
                blk.errors.append("%s %r is not `FILE` \"TEXT\"%s"
                                  % (field, v, ", none or not determined"
                                     if field == "Fix" else ""))
                continue
            frel, text = m.group(1), m.group(2)
            if field == "Fix" and (frel in (ROADMAP, DEFECTS)
                                   or is_archive(frel)):
                blk.errors.append("Fix points at %s, where no repair stands"
                                  % frel)
                continue
            status, lineno = find_anchor(files, frel, text)
            if status == "ok":
                blk.anchors[field] = (frel, lineno)
                if field == "Fix":
                    blk.fix = (frel, lineno)
                    if classify_location(files, frel, lineno, DEFECT) is None:
                        blk.errors.append("Fix under %s is neither a shipped "
                                          "directory nor docs/ or README.md"
                                          % frel)
            elif status != "multiple":
                blk.errors.append("%s %r in %s: %s" % (field, text, frel, {
                    "missing": "not " "found",
                    "no-file": "the file cannot " "be read",
                    "too-long": "longer than " "80 characters",
                    "too-short": "shorter than 20 characters and not " "a "
                                 "whole line",
                    "whitespace": "carries leading or " "trailing whitespace",
                }[status]))
            else:
                blk.errors.append("%s %r in %s: found more than once, on %s, so "
                                  "it is not an anchor"
                                  % (field, text, frel, lines_named(lineno)))
        status = blk.value("Status")
        if status is not None and status not in STATES[:4]:
            closing = closing_of(status)
            if closing is None or not status.startswith("Closed, "):
                blk.errors.append("Status %r is none of %s and none of the "
                                  "three closing forms of section D"
                                  % (status, ", ".join(STATES[:4])))
            else:
                n = len(blk.errors)
                check_closing(root, files, blk, closing)
                if len(blk.errors) == n:
                    blk.closing = closing
    block_by_title = {}
    for blk in blocks:
        block_by_title.setdefault(blk.title, blk)
    # the blocks against the branch point, section F: a block removed,
    # renamed or given another Should is named by a line of the fixed form
    # the branch adds to docs/roadmap.md, or the tool is red
    hold_blocks_to_branch_point(root, defects_rel, blocks, res)
    # the archives of the roadmap against the branch point, section F: one
    # that stood there and changed, was renamed or removed, or one added
    # that is not the roadmap of the branch point under the next number
    hold_archives_to_branch_point(root, res)
    # notes of things: the forms
    named = named_skills(files)
    roots = []    # things carrying ROOT
    calls = []    # (thing, skill called, mode, per); mode: reached, not
    #               reached, not stated, none (the third form), shared
    for rec in things:
        segs = rec["note"].split("; ") if rec["note"] != "" else []
        in_skill = skill_of(rec["file"]) is not None
        in_shared = rec["file"].startswith("shared/")
        i = 0
        while i < len(segs):
            s = segs[i]
            if s.startswith("calls "):
                nxt = segs[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(segs) else ""
                claim = nxt.startswith("with nobody there:")
                m = CALLS.match(s)
                if not m:
                    rec.errors.append("note %r does not carry the form "
                                      "'calls <skill>' exactly" % s)
                    i += 2 if claim else 1
                    continue
                skill = m.group(1)
                if not os.path.isdir(os.path.join(root, "skills", skill)):
                    rec.errors.append("calls %r, but skills/%s is not a "
                                      "directory" % (skill, skill))
                if in_shared:
                    if claim:
                        rec.errors.append("%r stands in shared text, where no "
                                          "reach claim can be made: the file "
                                          "stands in several skills, and the "
                                          "tool derives it" % nxt)
                    else:
                        calls.append((rec, skill, "shared", None))
                    i += 2 if claim else 1
                    continue
                if not in_skill:
                    rec.errors.append("a call note stands only on a thing "
                                      "under skills/ or shared/")
                    i += 2 if claim else 1
                    continue
                m2 = NOBODY.match(nxt)
                if nxt == NOBODY_NONE:
                    calls.append((rec, skill, "none", None))
                elif m2:
                    mode, per = m2.group(1), m2.group(2)
                    if per not in thing_names:
                        rec.errors.append("per %r names no thing" % per)
                    elif mode == "not stated" and NOT_STATED not in \
                            by_name[per]["note"].split("; "):
                        rec.errors.append("'not stated per' names %r, which "
                                          "does not carry %r"
                                          % (per, NOT_STATED))
                    calls.append((rec, skill, mode, per))
                else:
                    rec.errors.append("note %r does not carry one of the forms "
                                      "'calls <skill>; with nobody there: "
                                      "<reached|not reached|not stated> per "
                                      "<thing name>' or 'calls <skill>; %s' "
                                      "exactly" % ("; ".join(segs[i:i + 2]),
                                                   NOBODY_NONE))
                i += 2
                continue
            if s.startswith("with nobody there:"):
                rec.errors.append("%r stands without its 'calls <skill>' "
                                  "before it" % s)
            elif s == ROOT:
                roots.append(rec)
            elif s.startswith("enters the unattended"):
                rec.errors.append("note %r does not carry the form %r exactly"
                                  % (s, ROOT))
            elif s.startswith("does the work of"):
                m = DOES.match(s)
                if not m:
                    rec.errors.append("note %r does not carry the form 'does "
                                      "the work of <named skill>' exactly" % s)
                elif m.group(1) not in named:
                    rec.errors.append("does the work of %r, which the table "
                                      "under %r in docs/roadmap.md does not "
                                      "name" % (m.group(1), NAMED_HEADING))
            elif s.startswith("outcome with nobody there") and s != NOT_STATED:
                rec.errors.append("note %r does not carry the form %r exactly"
                                  % (s, NOT_STATED))
            i += 1
    # the root note stands on a thing and nowhere else
    for rec in records:
        if rec["type"] != "thing" and rec["note"] != "" \
                and ROOT in rec["note"].split("; "):
            rec.errors.append("carries %r, which only a thing may carry" % ROOT)
    # not-a-thing: part of names a thing
    for rec in records:
        if rec["type"] == "not-a-thing" and rec.part_of is not None \
                and rec.part_of not in thing_names:
            rec.errors.append("part of %r, which names no thing" % rec.part_of)
    # the skills the unattended mode reaches, computed from the roots: every
    # call claimed as reached in a reached skill, and every call in shared
    # text that a reached skill inserts, until nothing changes
    for rec in roots:
        if not rec.errors and skill_of(rec["file"]) is None:
            rec.errors.append("carries %r, but its site is not under skills/"
                              % ROOT)
    res.roots = [rec["name"] for rec in roots if not rec.errors]
    if not res.roots:
        res.table_errors.append("no thing carries %r, so the skills the "
                                "unattended mode reaches are not computed"
                                % ROOT)
    else:
        inserters = inserting_skills(files, root)
        reached = {}
        for rec in roots:
            if not rec.errors:
                reached.setdefault(skill_of(rec["file"]), "root: " + rec["name"])
        changed = True
        while changed:
            changed = False
            for rec, skill, mode, per in calls:
                if rec.errors or skill in reached:
                    continue
                if mode == "reached" and skill_of(rec["file"]) in reached:
                    reached[skill] = "per " + rec["name"]
                    changed = True
                elif mode == "shared":
                    via = sorted(s for s in inserters.get(rec["file"], ())
                                 if s in reached)
                    if via:
                        reached[skill] = "in %s, inserted into %s" % (
                            rec["file"], ", ".join(via))
                        changed = True
        res.reached = reached
        for rec, skill, mode, per in calls:
            if rec.errors or mode == "shared":
                continue
            s = skill_of(rec["file"])
            if mode == "none" and s in reached:
                rec.errors.append("%r stands in %s, which the mode reaches; a "
                                  "call before the boundary takes 'not reached "
                                  "per <thing name>'" % (NOBODY_NONE, s))
            elif mode != "none" and s not in reached:
                rec.errors.append("'with nobody there: %s per' stands in %s, "
                                  "which nothing reaches; the note takes %r"
                                  % (mode, s, NOBODY_NONE))
    # runs need their thing
    origin_main = resolve(root, "origin/main")
    tree_v = tree_version(root)
    for rec in records:
        if rec["type"] != "run":
            continue
        if rec["name"] in thing_names:
            if by_name[rec["name"]].errors:
                rec.errors.append("the thing named %r is rejected (line %d), "
                                  "so this run is rejected with it and covers "
                                  "nothing" % (rec["name"],
                                               by_name[rec["name"]].lineno))
        elif rec["name"] not in block_by_title:
            rec.errors.append("no thing named %r and no defect with that "
                              "title" % rec["name"])
        elif block_by_title[rec["name"]].errors:
            rec.errors.append("the defect %r is rejected (%s line %d), so this "
                              "run is rejected with it and covers nothing"
                              % (rec["name"], DEFECTS,
                                 block_by_title[rec["name"]].lineno))
        if rec["commit"] != "" and not rec.errors:
            h = resolve(root, rec["commit"])
            if h is None:
                rec.errors.append("commit %s does not resolve here"
                                  % rec["commit"])
            elif origin_main is None:
                rec.errors.append("origin/main is not here, so the commit "
                                  "cannot be checked against it")
            elif not g.is_ancestor(h, origin_main):
                rec.errors.append("commit %s is not an ancestor of origin/main"
                                  % rec["commit"])
    for rec in records:
        for e in rec.errors:
            res.broken.append((rec.lineno, e))
    for blk in blocks:
        for e in blk.errors:
            res.defect_errors.append((blk.lineno, e))
    # coverage by records that are not rejected, and by the anchors of the
    # blocks that are not rejected
    for rec in records:
        if rec.errors:
            continue
        for a, b in PAIRS:
            got = rec.anchors.get(b)
            if got and got[0] == "ok":
                res.covered_lines.setdefault(rec[a], set()).add(got[1])
    for blk in blocks:
        if blk.errors:
            continue
        for frel, lineno in blk.anchors.values():
            res.covered_lines.setdefault(frel, set()).add(lineno)
    # not-a-thing counts by reason
    for rec in records:
        if rec["type"] == "not-a-thing" and not rec.errors:
            head = "part of" if rec.part_of else rec["note"].split("; ")[0]
            res.reason_counts[head] = res.reason_counts.get(head, 0) + 1
    # part-of lines per thing
    part_of_lines = {}
    for rec in records:
        if rec["type"] == "not-a-thing" and not rec.errors and rec.part_of:
            part_of_lines.setdefault(rec.part_of, []).append(rec.site())
    # states
    things_by_name = {}
    valid_sp = set()
    for rec in things:
        if rec.errors:
            continue
        t = {"rec": rec, "name": rec["name"], "kind": rec["kind"],
             "site": rec.site(), "evidence": None, "state": None, "runs": [],
             "range": thing_range(files, rec, part_of_lines)}
        if rec["evidence_file"] != "":
            t["evidence"] = (rec["evidence_file"],
                             rec.anchors["evidence_anchor"][1])
            t["state"] = classify_location(files, rec["evidence_file"],
                                           rec.anchors["evidence_anchor"][1],
                                           rec["kind"])
        else:
            t["state"] = "Undetermined"
        t["closing"] = None
        things_by_name[t["name"]] = t
        res.things.append(t)
        if rec.parts[2] == STRAIGHT and is_unit_file(rec.site()[0]):
            u = files.unit_at(*rec.site())
            valid_sp.add((rec.site()[0], u["start"]))
    # the defects: a state per block, computed by section D off its Fix and
    # its closing, the lines a run is held against being the Fix line alone
    for blk in blocks:
        if blk.errors:
            continue
        d = {"rec": blk, "name": blk.title, "kind": DEFECT,
             "site": (DEFECTS, blk.lineno), "evidence": None, "state": None,
             "runs": [], "range": {}, "section": blk.section,
             "closing": blk.closing, "block": True,
             "not_stated": any(blk.value(f) == NOT_STATED_MARK
                               for f in ("Wrong", "Should"))}
        if blk.fix == FIX_NONE:
            d["state"] = "Recorded, not built"
        elif blk.fix == FIX_UNDETERMINED:
            d["state"] = "Undetermined"
        else:
            d["evidence"] = blk.fix
            d["state"] = classify_location(files, blk.fix[0], blk.fix[1],
                                           DEFECT)
            d["range"] = {blk.fix: "fix"}
        if blk.closing is not None:
            d["state"] = "Closed"
        things_by_name[blk.title] = d
        res.defects.append(d)
    # runs
    for rec in records:
        if rec["type"] != "run" or rec.errors:
            continue
        # every run without errors names a thing without errors, which the
        # block above guarantees, so this lookup cannot miss
        t = things_by_name[rec["name"]]
        r = {"rec": rec, "thing": t, "counts": False, "reason": "", "info": ""}
        t["runs"].append(r)
        res.runs.append(r)
        if t["state"] != "Built":
            res.broken.append((rec.lineno, "a run on %r, which is %s and "
                               "cannot be walked" % (t["name"], t["state"])))
            r["reason"] = "the thing is not built"
            continue
        # the last change of every line the thing stands on
        uncommitted = None
        per_hash = {}   # hash -> (time, rel, lineno, role)
        for (rel, n), role in sorted(t["range"].items()):
            b = g.blame_file(rel).get(n)
            if b is None or b[0] is None:
                uncommitted = (rel, n, role)
                break
            h, tm = b
            if h not in per_hash or tm > per_hash[h][0]:
                per_hash[h] = (tm, rel, n, role)
        if uncommitted is not None:
            r["reason"] = "%s:%d (%s) is uncommitted" % uncommitted
            continue
        failing = None   # (time, rel, n, role, hash)

        def where(h):
            tm, rel, n, role = per_hash[h]
            return "%s:%d (%s), %s at %s" % (rel, n, role, g.commit_date(h),
                                             h[:7])
        if rec["commit"] != "":
            run_c = resolve(root, rec["commit"])
            bad = [h for h in per_hash if not g.is_ancestor(h, run_c)]
            if not bad:
                r["counts"] = True
            else:
                failing = max(bad, key=lambda h: per_hash[h][0])
                r["reason"] = ("the run's commit %s does not contain the last "
                               "change to %s" % (rec["commit"], where(failing)))
        elif rec["version"] != "":
            vc = version_commit(root, rec["version"])
            if vc is None:
                res.broken.append((rec.lineno, "version %s was never "
                                   "introduced into .claude-plugin/plugin.json "
                                   "on this history" % rec["version"]))
                r["reason"] = "the version is not on this history"
            elif origin_main is not None and tree_v is not None \
                    and rec["version"] != tree_v \
                    and not g.is_ancestor(vc, origin_main):
                res.broken.append((rec.lineno, "version %s was introduced at "
                                   "%s, which is not on origin/main, and "
                                   ".claude-plugin/plugin.json carries %s: a "
                                   "squash merge lands only the version the "
                                   "branch ends on, so on main this run would "
                                   "name a version never introduced"
                                   % (rec["version"], vc[:7], tree_v)))
                r["reason"] = "the version would not survive the squash merge"
            else:
                early = False
                if origin_main is not None and tree_v is not None \
                        and rec["version"] == tree_v \
                        and not g.is_ancestor(vc, origin_main):
                    # the version is this branch's own: the run's row
                    # against the last change to every line of the thing,
                    # since 14 October 2026 - section C of the header
                    b = g.blame_file(table_rel).get(rec.lineno)
                    row = None if b is None or b[0] is None else b[0]
                    for h in sorted(per_hash, key=lambda h: -per_hash[h][0]):
                        tm, hrel, hn, hrole = per_hash[h]
                        if row is not None and not g.is_ancestor(h, row):
                            res.broken.append((rec.lineno, "recorded too "
                                               "early: the row came into the "
                                               "table at %s (%s), which does "
                                               "not contain the last change "
                                               "to %s; a run under the "
                                               "version this branch "
                                               "introduces is recorded only "
                                               "at the branch's final state, "
                                               "and on main after the squash "
                                               "merge this could not be seen"
                                               % (row[:7], g.commit_date(row),
                                                  where(h))))
                            early = True
                            break
                if early:
                    r["reason"] = ("the run was recorded before the last "
                                   "change to its thing")
                    continue
                bad = [h for h in per_hash if not g.is_ancestor(h, vc)]
                if not bad:
                    r["counts"] = True
                else:
                    failing = max(bad, key=lambda h: per_hash[h][0])
                    r["reason"] = ("version %s (introduced at %s) predates the "
                                   "last change to %s"
                                   % (rec["version"], vc[:7], where(failing)))
        else:
            r["reason"] = "the version of the run is not recorded"
        if failing is not None:
            _, frel, fn, _ = per_hash[failing]
            c = blame_copy(root, frel, fn)
            if c is not None and c[0] != failing:
                r["info"] = ("copy detection attributes %s:%d to %s (%s) in %s"
                             % (frel, fn, c[0][:7], g.commit_date(c[0]), c[1]))
    for d in res.defects:
        if d["state"] == "Built":
            d["state"] = ("Walked" if any(r["counts"] for r in d["runs"])
                          else "Built, never walked")
        key = (d["state"], DEFECT)
        res.counts[key] = res.counts.get(key, 0) + 1
        said = d["rec"].value("Status")
        if d["closing"] is None and said != d["state"]:
            res.defect_errors.append((d["rec"].lineno, "the block %r says %r, "
                                      "and the tool computes %s" % (
                                          d["name"], said, d["state"])))
    for t in res.things:
        if t["state"] == "Built":
            t["state"] = ("Walked" if any(r["counts"] for r in t["runs"])
                          else "Built, never walked")
        key = (t["state"], t["kind"])
        res.counts[key] = res.counts.get(key, 0) + 1
        rel = t["site"][0]
        tally(res, rel, "things")
        if t["rec"].parts[2] == STRAIGHT:
            tally(res, rel, "straight paths")
        elif t["kind"] in ("skill branch", "shared text", "output style"):
            tally(res, rel, "branches")
        else:
            tally(res, rel, "other kinds")
        calls, does, not_stated = note_forms(t["rec"]["note"])
        tally(res, rel, "calls", calls)
        tally(res, rel, "does the work of", does)
        tally(res, rel, NOT_STATED, not_stated)
    for rec in records:
        if rec.errors:
            continue
        if rec["type"] == "not-a-thing":
            tally(res, rec["file"], "not-a-thing")
    # units: every one has a straight path and an anchorable line
    for rel in unit_files(files, root, only):
        text, lines = files.get(rel)
        for u in files.units(rel)[0]:
            heading = u["part"]
            at = u["hline"] or u["start"]
            if (rel, u["start"]) not in valid_sp:
                res.no_straight_path.append((rel, at, heading))
            if not any(anchorable(text, lines[n - 1])
                       for n in range(u["start"], u["end"] + 1)):
                res.unanchorable_units.append((rel, at, heading))
    # coverage
    items = search_set(files, root, only)
    for rel, first, last, text in items:
        got = files.get(rel)
        if first == last and not anchorable(got[0], text):
            res.unanchorable.append((rel, first, text))
            continue
        res.set_size += 1
        covered = res.covered_lines.get(rel, set())
        if not any(first <= n <= last for n in covered):
            res.uncovered.append((rel, first, last, text))
    return res


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- output

def render(res):
    out = []
    if res.refused:
        out.append("REFUSED: " + res.refused)
        return "\n".join(out) + "\n"
    out.append("BROKEN RECORDS: %d" % res.broken_count())
    for e in res.table_errors:
        out.append("  " + e)
    for lineno, e in res.broken:
        out.append("  line %d: %s" % (lineno, e))
    for lineno, e in res.defect_errors:
        out.append("  %s:%d: %s" % (DEFECTS, lineno, e))
    for e in res.guard_errors:
        out.append("  against the branch point: " + e)
    for e in res.archive_errors:
        out.append("  archives: " + e)
    out.append("")
    out.append("ROOTS OF THE UNATTENDED MODE (things carrying %r): %d"
               % (ROOT, len(res.roots)))
    for name in res.roots:
        out.append("  " + name)
    if res.reached is None:
        out.append("SKILLS THE UNATTENDED MODE REACHES: not computed, no root")
    else:
        out.append("SKILLS THE UNATTENDED MODE REACHES: %d" % len(res.reached))
        for skill in sorted(res.reached):
            out.append("  %-18s %s" % (skill, res.reached[skill]))
    out.append("")
    out.append("STATES, by file and kind (evidence that decided each)")
    groups = {}
    for t in res.things:
        gfile = t["evidence"][0] if t["evidence"] else t["site"][0]
        groups.setdefault((gfile, t["kind"]), []).append(t)
    for (gfile, kind) in sorted(groups):
        out.append("  %s / %s" % (gfile, kind))
        for t in sorted(groups[(gfile, kind)], key=lambda t: t["name"]):
            ev = ("%s:%d" % t["evidence"]) if t["evidence"] else "no evidence"
            out.append("    %-22s %s  (%s)" % (t["state"], t["name"], ev))
    out.append("")
    out.append("DEFECTS of %s, by section (the state of each, counted per "
               "section; the Fix that decided it)" % DEFECTS)
    for _, section in res.sections:
        ds = [d for d in res.defects if d["section"] == section]
        counted = ", ".join("%s %d" % (st, sum(1 for d in ds if d["state"] == st))
                            for st in STATES
                            if any(d["state"] == st for d in ds))
        out.append("  %s: %d%s" % (section, len(ds),
                                   " (" + counted + ")" if counted else ""))
        for d in sorted(ds, key=lambda d: d["rec"].lineno):
            if d["state"] == "Closed":
                continue
            fix = ("%s:%d" % d["evidence"]) if d["evidence"] \
                else d["rec"].fix
            out.append("    %-22s %s  (%s)" % (d["state"], d["name"], fix))
    out.append("")
    closed = [d for d in res.defects if d["state"] == "Closed"]
    out.append("CLOSED DEFECTS (reason and proof, in no list of open things): %d"
               % len(closed))
    for d in sorted(closed, key=lambda d: d["name"]):
        reason, rel, text, commit = d["closing"]
        out.append("  " + d["name"])
        out.append("    %s: %s %r%s  (%s:%d)" % (
            reason, rel, text, ", removed at " + commit if commit else "",
            DEFECTS, d["rec"].lineno))
    out.append("")
    unsorted = [d for d in res.defects if d["section"] == NOT_YET_SORTED]
    out.append("DEFECTS UNDER %s (not yet sorted into a milestone): %d"
               % (NOT_YET_SORTED, len(unsorted)))
    for d in unsorted:
        out.append("  " + d["name"])
    out.append("")
    unstated = [d for d in res.defects if d["not_stated"]]
    out.append("DEFECTS WITH %r IN Wrong OR Should (no text of the roadmap "
               "filled it): %d" % (NOT_STATED_MARK, len(unstated)))
    for d in unstated:
        out.append("  " + d["name"])
    out.append("")
    out.append("BLOCKS HELD AGAINST THE BRANCH POINT (git merge-base HEAD "
               "origin/main; a change needs its line in %s)" % ROADMAP)
    g = res.guard or {}
    if g.get("note"):
        out.append("  " + g["note"])
    elif g:
        out.append("  branch point %s: blocks removed %d, renamed %d, Should "
                   "changed %d, added %d; lines of the fixed forms added to "
                   "%s: %d" % (g["base"][:7], len(g["removed"]),
                               len(g["renamed"]), len(g["changed"]),
                               len(g["added"]), ROADMAP, g["lines"]))
        for t in g["removed"]:
            out.append("    removed         " + t)
        for old, new in g["renamed"]:
            out.append("    renamed         %s%s%s" % (old, RENAME_SEP, new))
        for t in g["changed"]:
            out.append("    Should changed  " + t)
    out.append("")
    out.append("ARCHIVES OF THE ROADMAP HELD AGAINST THE BRANCH POINT (git "
               "merge-base HEAD origin/main; an archive never changes)")
    a = res.archives or {}
    if a.get("note"):
        out.append("  " + a["note"])
    elif a:
        out.append("  branch point %s: archives standing there %d, added by "
                   "this branch %d" % (a["base"][:7], len(a["held"]),
                                       len(a["added"])))
        for rel in a["held"]:
            out.append("    held            " + rel)
        for rel in a["added"]:
            out.append("    added           " + rel)
    out.append("")
    out.append("RUNS THAT DO NOT COUNT")
    n = 0
    for r in res.runs:
        if r["counts"]:
            continue
        n += 1
        rec = r["rec"]
        out.append("  line %d, %s on %s: %s" % (rec.lineno, rec["date"],
                                                 r["thing"]["name"], r["reason"]))
        if r["info"]:
            out.append("    " + r["info"])
    if n == 0:
        out.append("  none")
    out.append("")
    out.append("COUNTS per state and kind")
    for state in STATES:
        total = sum(v for (s, k), v in res.counts.items() if s == state)
        out.append("  %-22s %d" % (state, total))
        for kind in KINDS + (DEFECT,):
            v = res.counts.get((state, kind), 0)
            if v:
                out.append("    %-20s %d" % (kind, v))
    out.append("")
    out.append("COUNTS by site file (records that are not rejected)")
    total = dict((k, 0) for k in TALLY_KEYS)
    for rel in sorted(res.by_file):
        d = res.by_file[rel]
        for k in TALLY_KEYS:
            total[k] += d[k]
    for rel, d in sorted(res.by_file.items()) + [("all files", total)]:
        out.append("  %s" % rel)
        out.append("    things %d: straight paths %d, branches %d, other "
                   "kinds %d" % (d["things"], d["straight paths"],
                                 d["branches"], d["other kinds"]))
        out.append("    notes: calls %d, does the work of %d, %s %d"
                   % (d["calls"], d["does the work of"], NOT_STATED,
                      d[NOT_STATED]))
        out.append("    not-a-thing %d" % d["not-a-thing"])
    out.append("")
    out.append("NOT-A-THING RECORDS by reason: %d"
               % sum(res.reason_counts.values()))
    for reason in REASONS:
        v = res.reason_counts.get(reason, 0)
        if v:
            out.append("  %-20s %d" % (reason, v))
    out.append("")
    out.append("LINES OF THE SEARCH SET THAT CAN CARRY NO UNIQUE ANCHOR "
               "(they leave the set): %d" % len(res.unanchorable))
    for rel, lineno, text in res.unanchorable:
        out.append("  %s:%d: %s" % (rel, lineno, text.strip()[:70]))
    for rel, lineno, heading in res.unanchorable_units:
        out.append("  %s:%d: unit %r has no anchorable line at all"
                   % (rel, lineno, heading))
    out.append("")
    out.append("UNITS WITHOUT A STRAIGHT PATH: %d" % len(res.no_straight_path))
    for rel, lineno, heading in res.no_straight_path:
        out.append("  %s:%d: %s" % (rel, lineno, heading))
    out.append("")
    out.append("UNCOVERED LINES OF THE SEARCH SET: %d of %d"
               % (len(res.uncovered), res.set_size))
    for rel, first, last, text in res.uncovered:
        where = "%d" % first if first == last else "%d-%d" % (first, last)
        out.append("  %s:%s: %s" % (rel, where, text.strip()[:70]))
    return "\n".join(out) + "\n"


def exit_code(res):
    if res.refused or res.broken_count():
        return 2
    if res.uncovered or res.no_straight_path:
        return 1
    return 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- self-test

def message_templates(path):
    """(shape, line, template) for every message this tool can produce, read
    off its own source: the strings handed to rec.errors.append,
    res.broken.append, res.table_errors.append, res.guard_errors.append,
    res.archive_errors.append and errors.append, assigned to
    a run's reason or info or to res.refused, printed to stderr, or raised as
    RuntimeError. The self-test's own printing is left out. A string inside a
    dict that a message picks its ending from is a fragment, matched inside a
    message; every other template is matched whole."""
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        tree = ast.parse(f.read())

    def strings(node):
        if isinstance(node, ast.Constant) and isinstance(node.value, str):
            return [("whole", node.lineno, node.value)]
        if isinstance(node, ast.BinOp) and isinstance(node.op, ast.Mod):
            out = strings(node.left)
            for sub in ast.walk(node.right):
                if isinstance(sub, ast.Subscript) \
                        and isinstance(sub.value, ast.Dict):
                    out += [("fragment", v.lineno, v.value)
                            for v in sub.value.values
                            if isinstance(v, ast.Constant)]
            return out
        if isinstance(node, ast.BinOp) and isinstance(node.op, ast.Add):
            left, right = strings(node.left), strings(node.right)
            if len(left) == 1 and len(right) == 1:
                return [("whole", left[0][1], left[0][2] + right[0][2])]
            return left + right
        if isinstance(node, ast.Tuple):
            return [s for e in node.elts for s in strings(e)]
        return []

    found = []
    for top in tree.body:
        if not isinstance(top, ast.FunctionDef) or top.name == "selftest":
            continue
        for node in ast.walk(top):
            if isinstance(node, ast.Call) \
                    and isinstance(node.func, ast.Attribute) \
                    and node.func.attr == "append":
                owner = node.func.value
                name = owner.attr if isinstance(owner, ast.Attribute) \
                    else getattr(owner, "id", "")
                if name in ("errors", "broken", "table_errors",
                            "guard_errors", "archive_errors"):
                    for a in node.args:
                        found += strings(a)
            elif isinstance(node, ast.Assign):
                target = node.targets[0]
                key = None
                if isinstance(target, ast.Subscript) \
                        and isinstance(target.slice, ast.Constant):
                    key = target.slice.value
                elif isinstance(target, ast.Attribute):
                    key = target.attr
                if key in ("reason", "info", "refused"):
                    found += strings(node.value)
            elif isinstance(node, ast.Call) \
                    and getattr(node.func, "id", "") == "print" \
                    and any(k.arg == "file" for k in node.keywords):
                for a in node.args:
                    found += strings(a)
            elif isinstance(node, ast.Raise) \
                    and isinstance(node.exc, ast.Call) \
                    and getattr(node.exc.func, "id", "") == "RuntimeError":
                for a in node.exc.args:
                    found += strings(a)
    return found


def template_regex(template):
    """The template with every %r, %s and %d standing for anything."""
    parts = re.split(r"%-?\d*[rsd]", template)
    return re.compile("(?s)" + ".*".join(re.escape(p) for p in parts))


def selftest():
    """Builds a temporary repository and produces every red outcome once,
    each beside its green counterpart. Prints one line per case, then which
    of the messages the tool can produce no case asserted."""
    failures = []
    cases = []
    asserted = []   # every message a case read
    produced = []   # every message the analyses of this self-test produced

    def case(name, red, green):
        ok = red and green
        cases.append(name)
        print("%s  %s: red %s, green %s" % ("ok " if ok else "BAD", name,
                                            "seen" if red else "MISSING",
                                            "seen" if green else "MISSING"))
        if not ok:
            failures.append(name)

    def messages_of(res):
        out = [res.refused] if res.refused else []
        out += res.table_errors
        out += [e for _, e in res.broken]
        out += [e for _, e in res.defect_errors]
        out += res.guard_errors
        out += res.archive_errors
        for r in res.runs:
            out += [r[k] for k in ("reason", "info") if r[k]]
        return out

    def collect(res):
        produced.extend(messages_of(res))
        return res

    def has(res, sub):
        """Whether a result carries a message with this text; the text is
        counted as asserted by a case."""
        asserted.append(sub)
        return any(sub in m for m in messages_of(res))

    def run_git(root, *args, date=None):
        env = dict(os.environ)
        env.update({"GIT_AUTHOR_NAME": "t", "GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL": "t@t",
                    "GIT_COMMITTER_NAME": "t", "GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL": "t@t"})
        if date:
            env["GIT_AUTHOR_DATE"] = date
            env["GIT_COMMITTER_DATE"] = date
        p = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root] + list(args), env=env,
                           capture_output=True, text=True)
        if p.returncode != 0:
            raise RuntimeError("git %s: %s" % (" ".join(args), p.stderr))
        return p.stdout.strip()

    def write(root, rel, text):
        path = os.path.join(root, rel)
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        with open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write(text)

    def plugin(v):
        return '{\n  "name": "t",\n  "version": "%s",\n  "skills": []\n}\n' % v

    def files_of(root, rel):
        with open(os.path.join(root, rel), encoding="utf-8") as f:
            return f.read().split("\n")

    insert = "!`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/devloop-text inserted`"
    alpha_tail = ("Say what you would do, and let the user decide how.\n\n"
                  "## Second\n\nA second unit that says one thing and decides "
                  "nothing.\n" + insert + "\n\n")
    third = "## Third\n\nThe third unit, stable from the first commit on.\n"
    # a unit copied word for word from the extra file into the skill in c2,
    # its heading long enough for git's copy detection, which looks only at
    # files the same commit changes, so c2 changes the extra file too
    fourth_head = "Fourth, copied word for word from the extra file into the skill later"
    fourth = "## " + fourth_head + "\n\nThe extra file carries the same unit.\n"

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        root = os.path.join(tmp, "repo")
        os.makedirs(root)
        run_git(root, "init", "-q", "-b", "main")
        write(root, ".claude-plugin/plugin.json", plugin("0.1.0"))
        write(root, "skills/alpha/SKILL.md",
              "# Alpha\n\nIf the sky is blue, say that it is blue today.\n"
              "Where the sky is grey, say nothing about the weather.\n"
              "Plain prose that decides nothing at all here.\n"
              "Unless it rains, carry on with the ordinary work.\n" + alpha_tail
              + third)
        write(root, "skills/beta/SKILL.md", "# Beta\n")
        write(root, "skills/gamma/SKILL.md",
              "# Gamma\n\n```markdown\n## Fake\n```\n\n"
              "If a fence stands here, the heading inside it is not a unit.\n")
        write(root, "skills/epsilon/SKILL.md",
              "# Epsilon\n\n## First\n\n### Twice\n\nText under the first "
              "twice.\n\n## Second\n\n### Twice\n\nText under the second "
              "twice.\n")
        write(root, "skills/alpha/LOGIC.md",
              "# Alpha\n\nIf the extra file says so, do that too.\n\n" + fourth)
        # zeta: two units whose every line repeats, so none can carry an
        # anchor; theta: lines for notes written wrongly; misc: a directory
        # the tool classifies nowhere; two shared files for rejected records
        write(root, "skills/zeta/SKILL.md",
              "# Zeta\n\n## Same\n\nsame text\n\n## Same\n\nsame text\n")
        write(root, "skills/theta/SKILL.md", "# Theta\n\n" + "".join(
            "Theta line %d, a fixture for a note written wrongly.\n" % i
            for i in range(1, 9)))
        write(root, "misc/elsewhere.md", "A file the tool classifies nowhere.\n")
        write(root, "shared/lines.md", "".join(
            "Fixture line %02d for a record the tool rejects.\n" % i
            for i in range(1, 7)) + "A line of more than eighty characters, "
            "so that an anchor taking the whole of it is too long to be one.\n")
        write(root, "shared/kind.md", "A shared file whose straight path is "
              "recorded with the wrong kind.\n")
        write(root, "shared/note.md",
              "When somebody writes in German, answer in German.\n")
        write(root, "shared/inserted.md", "Inserted text, first wording.\n")
        # an output style, a unit file outside skills/ and shared/ since 15
        # October 2026: its first unit holds the frontmatter, a line with a
        # search word covered as part of the straight path and one no record
        # covers; its second unit a straight path of the wrong kind
        write(root, "output-styles/style.md",
              "---\nname: t\nforce-for-plugin: true\n---\n\n# Style\n\n"
              "Where a skill says to say it, say it as text of the message.\n"
              "Plain prose of the style, deciding nothing.\n"
              "Unless a skill owes it, nothing changes here.\n\n"
              "## Second\n\nA second unit of the style.\n")
        write(root, "hooks/guard.sh",
              "#!/bin/sh\n[ -d docs/agents ] || exit 0\n"
              "echo blocked >&2\nexit 2\nexit 0\nexit 0\n")
        write(root, "docs/skill-conventions.md",
              "# Conventions\n\n## Before a handover, run these\n\n"
              "One check, silent when green:\n\n"
              "    ls skills/ | grep -c nothing-of-that-name\n\n"
              "## Something else\n\nText.\n")
        roadmap_c1 = (
              "# Roadmap\n\n## Known gaps\n\n"
              "- **A defect measured on 2026-01-06, on the first version.**\n"
              "  Recorded, not built.\n"
              "- **A run of alpha on 2026-01-11, on the second version.**\n"
              "- **A run of the check after its line changed, on the first version.**\n"
              "- **A run of the check after its line changed, on the second version.**\n"
              "- **A run of alpha without a version.**\n"
              "- **A run on the shared note.**\n"
              "- **A run that names a commit of main.**\n"
              "- **A run that names a commit of a side branch.**\n"
              "- **A run of blue before the rain line changed.**\n"
              "- **A run of blue after the rain line changed.**\n"
              "- **A run of the first unit before it changed.**\n"
              "- **A run of the first unit after it changed.**\n"
              "- **A run of the second unit before its insert changed.**\n"
              "- **A run of the third unit, which never changed.**\n"
              "- **A run on a thing nobody recorded.**\n"
              "- **A defect answered by a check.**\n"
              "- **A defect answered a section further on.**\n"
              "- **A run on the defect answered by a check.**\n"
              "- **A run on the defect that is not built.**\n"
              "- **A defect with evidence elsewhere.**\n"
              "- **A run on a thing that is rejected.**\n"
              "- **A run keyed to a thing whose anchor is missing.**\n"
              "- **A run naming a commit that does not resolve.**\n"
              "- **A run naming the first commit.**\n"
              "- **A run naming a version never introduced.**\n"
              "- **A run naming the first version a branch introduces.**\n"
              "- **A run naming the last version a branch introduces.**\n"
              "- **A run with a bad date.**\n"
              "- **A run with a bad source.**\n"
              "- **A run with both a version and a commit.**\n"
              "- **A run of source none with a version.**\n"
              "- **A run of source entry with neither.**\n"
              "- **A run naming a commit with source log.**\n"
              "- **A run of the fourth unit, copied from the extra file.**\n"
              "- **A run recorded before a later commit changed its thing.**\n"
              "- **A run recorded after the last change to its thing.**\n"
              "- **A head that stands twice.**\n"
              "- **A head that stands twice.**\n"
              "- **A head that\n  wraps.**\n"
              "- **A defect answered by the output style.**\n\n"
              "## Named, not built as skills\n\n"
              "| Skill | Description | Origin |\n|---|---|---|\n"
              "| `implement-ticket` | Build one task | test |\n"
              "| `beta` | A name whose directory exists | test |\n"
              "| `delta` | A name whose directory does not exist | test |\n"
              "| `omega-skill` | A row that no record covers | test |\n\n"
